on vitiani pallini biffoni todaro invernici cenci vascularisation kukk lymboussaki taira kaipainen jeltsh joukov vegfr zagzag lukyanov esencay kipps saur seidler algul senekowitsch schmidtke mansel cabioglu doucet yavuz igci yildirim metast rempel dudas sdf1 drabik kutsch tousson benveniste astroglioma woerner warrington kalkanis decarvalho sdf marhuenda campone vallette peichev naiyer vegfr torrente belicchi mamchaoui furling mouly maitland hakomori glycoconj taieb maresca garmy fantini yahi ganglioside sprick bree scopelliti hoek hakomori sphingo glyco tsuruoka hakomori gm3 ganglioside fua169 ohkawa miyata hamamura gd3 birks danquah vlasak gorecki pilkington gd3 rutella bonanno procoli mariotti corallo prisco mou karbanova missol kolka lorra janich hollerova prominin histochem cytochem florek marzesco freud ehninger huttner prominin blakely penttila shukalyuk rappa fodstad lorico prominin soltysova altanerova altaner','Abstract The CD133 glycoprotein is a controversial cancer stem cell marker in the field of neuro‐oncology, based largely on the now considerable experimental evidence for the existence of both CD133+ve and CD133−ve populations as tumour‐initiating cells. It is thought that decreasing oxygen tension enhances the complex regulation and phenotype of CD133 in glioma. In light of these ideologies, establishing the precise functional role of CD133 is becoming increasingly critical. In this article, we review the complex regulation of CD133 and its extracellular epitope AC133, and associated alterations, to tumour cell behaviour by hypoxia. Furthermore, its role in functional modulation of tumours, rather than determination of a specific stem cell type is therefore alluded to, while evidence for and against its ability as a cancer stem cell marker in primary brain tumours, is critically evaluated. Thus, the suggestion that CD133 may be a central ‘holy grail’ in identifying core cells for propagation of malignant glial neoplasms seems increasingly less convincing. It remains to be seen, however, whether CD133 is randomly expressed on such brain tumour cell populations or whether it is of major significance to brain biological behaviour. Occurrence of AC133/CD133 in normal and tumour tissues CD133 and its extracellular epitope AC133 have evoked controversy in the proposal of them being defined as cancer stem cell markers, in the field of neuro‐oncology. In brain tumours, expression of AC133 has been said to encompass a subset of stem‐like tumour cells [supposed cancer stem cells (CSCs)], which are therapy‐resistant, and being the possible driving force of a highly tumourigenic phenotype. The phrase ‘cancer stem cell’ implies overlapping functional properties common to normal stem cells (NSCs), specifically extensive self‐renewal, and lineage‐specific differentiation. CD133 (Prominin‐1) is a transmembrane glycoprotein, encoded by the PROM1 gene 1 , consisting of an extracellular N‐terminus, a cytoplasmic C‐terminus (containing 5 tyrosine residues including a tyrosine phosphorylation consensus site), 2 small cysteine‐rich cytoplasmic loops and 2 very large extracellular loops each containing 4 consensus sequences for N‐linked glycosylation 2 (Fig. 1 ). cDNA cloning and tissue distribution studies have identified CD133 as a single‐chain polypeptide of 865 amino acids (aa) with a molecular weight of 120 kDa 2 . Other features revealed by Procite analysis, include a putative signalling peptide at the N‐terminus 3 . It has been further reported that the CD133 protein is phosphorylated within its C‐terminus in a Src kinase‐dependent manner; thus, phosphorylation of CD133 tyrosine‐828 may mediate its co‐operation with SH2‐domain‐containing proteins, which may be implicated in intracellular signalling cascades 3 . Although CD133 was the earliest member of the Prominin family of pentaspan membrane proteins to be identified, the exact function of these proteins remains uncertain. Expression of Prominins is restricted to plasma membrane protrusions and microvilli; consequently, their name originates from the latin prominere – ‘to be prominent’ 4 . CD133 has not been reported to have any enzymatic activity, intracellular binding proteins, or known extracellular ligands. Restricted expression of CD133 to plasma membrane protrusions 5 , 6 plus specific interactions with cholesterol‐based lipid rafts 7 suggests that CD133 may function as a regulator of plasma membrane topology 1 , further suggesting a role of the protein in cell polarity and cell–cell and cell–extracellular matrix‐mediated migration 8 . Figure 1. Open in a new tab Schematic representation of the transmembrane glycoprotein, CD133. The Transmembrane glycoprotein CD133, consisting of an extracellular N‐terminus, a cytoplasmic C‐terminus, 2 small cysteine rich cytoplasmic loops and 2 very large extracellular loops each containing 4 potential sites for N‐linked glycosylation. In 1997, Yin et al . produced an innovative monoclonal antibody, which identified the AC133 antigen, an extracellular, glycosylation‐reliant epitope of CD133, whose restricted expression in CD34+ve progenitor cell populations from adult blood, foetal liver cells and bone marrow suggested a function for the protein as a marker of haematopoietic progenitor cells 9 . In 2003, Singh et al . documented the first expression of AC133‐positive cells in glioma 10 ; cells positive for AC133 were cultured under defined stem cell conditions, cultured as neurospheres, displayed extensive self‐renewal, and upon differentiation, displayed markers reminiscent of astrocytic, oligodendroglial and neuronal lineage 10 . Antibodies directed against AC133 have frequently been utilized to isolate and enrich highly tumourigenic and therapy‐resistant subpopulations in various types of brain tumour 11 , 12 . In gliomas, the most common and most aggressive primary brain tumour, AC133‐expression in situ has been shown to increase with tumour malignancy and to be associated with poor patient prognosis 13 , indicating a role for AC133‐positive glioma cells in tumour expansion. The fundamental roles of AC133 and CD133 are further complicated by the complex tissue and micro‐environment‐dependent expression of CD133. Its expression involves at present, 3, varying levels of complexity: (i) Transcription of CD133 can be initiated at different, tissue‐specific promoters, producing numerous alternatively spliced transcripts, which manipulate cell distribution of the protein 14 , 15 ; (ii) CD133 protein has several possible glycosylation sites, permitting differential glycosylation 9 ; (iii) low oxygen tensions increase AC133 epitope rather than CD133 mRNA or protein in GBM 16 . We and others have recently found that higher migratory potential is inversely correlated with CD133 expression, suggesting that its expression does not facilitate a role in cell invasion 17 , 18 . According to the current hypothesis held by Prestegarden et al ., only CSCs (or tumour‐initiating cells) are capable of tumour cell self‐renewal, suggesting that they must acquire a migratory potential 19 . A model has therefore been proposed in which the CD133+ve population represents the non‐invasive GBM stem cell population with the inherent potential to shift reversibly between migratory and stationary phenotypes 18 . This switching associated with reversible loss and gain of CD133 marker expression has been seen to be associated with migratory proteins cysteine cathepsins S and B 18 . Cysteine cathepsins represent the largest family of lysosomal enzymes, are over‐expressed in a variety of tumour tissue cells and have been reported to be mediators of glioma cell invasion 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 , 22 . Positive correlation between CD133−ve cells and cysteine cathepsins has been reported, and further explained by downregulation of cathepsin inhibitors during the process of stem cell differentiation; moreover, cathepsin B was seen to be upregulated following differentiation of monocytes into tissue macrophages 18 , 23 . Ardebili et al . used 6 GBM tumour samples to show that CD133+ve cells contained significantly lower levels of cathepsins B, L and S activities than did the CD133−ve cell fraction. Together, these results suggest that GBM CSCs are less invasive than their progenitor counterparts, which have lost CD133 expression, but acquired migratory properties by activation of proteolytic enzymes, including cysteine cathepsins 18 . The belief that CD133 in its glycosylated form may act as a universal marker of brain tumour stem cells has been met with a high degree of controversy in the neuro‐oncology research community as there is now considerable experimental evidence for the existence of both AC133+ve and AC133−ve populations as tumour‐initiating cells 24 , 25 . In addition, it is hypothesized that increasing oxygen tension enhances the complex regulation and stem cell‐like phenotype of CD133 cells in glioma 16 , 17 . In light of these findings, establishing the precise biological and functional role of CD133 is of major importance. In this article, we review the complex regulation of CD133 and its extracellular epitope AC133, by hypoxia, as well as associated alterations in tumour cell biological and molecular behaviour. Furthermore, its role in modulation, rather than determination of a specific stem cell type is therefore alluded to, while evidence for and against its ability as a cancer stem cell marker in primary brain tumours is critically evaluated. The complex relationship of AC133/CD133 with hypoxia Local oxygen concentrations can directly influence stem cell self‐renewal, maintaining growth of tumours. An attractive notion is that stem cells, particularly in long‐lived animals, benefit from residing in hypoxic niches where oxidative DNA damage is possibly reduced 26 . Poor patient prognosis is generally correlated with tumour hypoxia, as low oxygen levels reduce effectiveness of radiotherapy 27 . Advancement of cancer by hypoxic micro‐environments occurs by initiation of transcription factors promoting cell survival, motility and tumour angiogenesis. Adaption to hypoxia at the cellular level is regulated predominately by HIF1α and HIF2α (also known as EPAS1) 28 . HIFs are heterodimeric proteins belonging to the basic helix‐loop‐helix (bHLH)/Per‐ARNT‐Sim (PAS) domain family of transcription factors 29 . The HIFα oxygen‐regulated subunit (HIF1α and HIF2α) activates gene expression via formation of a dimeric complex with constitutively expressed HIFβ subunit (also known as aryl hydrocarbon receptor nuclear translocator, ARNT), which subsequently binds to hypoxia response elements (HREs) within target genes 30 , see Fig. 2 . HIF target genes promote cells to survive oxygen deprivation by giving oxygen‐independent means of ATP production or by inhibiting hypoxia‐inducible apoptosis. The carboxy‐terminal half of HIF1α and HIF2α contain domains, which regulate its stability and transcriptional activity, in an oxygen‐dependent manner 31 . HIF1α and HIF2α are closely related, having 48% primary amino acid sequence identity, emphasized by the ability of both proteins to heterodimerize with the subsequent nuclear‐bound HIFβ subunit, and to bind to similar hypoxia regulatory element sequences 32 initiating full transcription of downstream target genes. Sequence conservation between HIF1α and HIF2α is high in bHLH and PAS regions in N termini and in oxygen‐regulated degradation domains. Sequence identity is in transactivation domains at the very end of the C terminal of both HIF1α and HIF2α proteins. Importantly, both HIF1α and HIF2α have different transcriptional functions, which are essential for generating transcriptional diversity 31 . Despite these similarities, expression profiling and functional studies have revealed that HIF‐ α subunits regulate both shared and unique target genes 33 . For instance, HIF‐1 α uniquely stimulates expression of glycolytic enzymes, such as phosphoglycerate kinase and lactate dehydrogenase‐A, carbonic anhydrase‐9, and pro‐apoptotic gene BNIP‐3 32 , 34 , 35 , 36 . In contrast, embryonic transcription factor Oct‐4 ( Pou5f1 , Oct‐3/4 ), CYCLIN D1 , TWIST1 , c‐MYC, transforming growth factor‐ α (TGF‐α), and erythropoietin (EPO) are upregulated under hypoxia in a HIF2 α ‐dependent manner 32 , 37 , 38 , 39 , 40 , indicating that HIF2α plays an essential role in maintaining stem cell control. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Schematic representation of the hypoxia inducible factor pathway: The regulation and activation of hypoxia inducible factor target gene expression: Under normoxic conditions, in the presence of oxygen, the HIFα subunit is hydroxylated by proline hydroxylase (PHD). The hydroxylated (OH) HIFα is recognised by a product of the von Hippel‐Lindau tumour suppressor gene (VHL) which tags the HIFα‐OH with polyubiquitin for subsequent degradation by the ubiquitin‐proteosome pathway. In contrast, in the absence of oxygen or levels below that of 8% O₂, proline hydroxylation does not occur, therefore enabling the dimerisation of the HIFα and HIFβ subunits. Upon translocation of HIFα and HIFβ subunits to the cell nucleus, the subunits interact with hypoxia regulatory elements (HREs), recruiting the transcriptional co‐activator p300, activating the full transcription of downstream genes which regulate cell survival, motility, metabolism and angiogenesis. Expression of HIF2α is restricted to cells of a distinct type, such as vascular endothelial cells and neural crest cell derivatives 41 , but few studies have been completed to determine its role in tumour growth 32 , 34 , 39 . In spite of this, in recent studies, it has become obvious that the two proteins, although highly homologous, have specific target genes and therefore participate in many non‐overlapping biological mechanisms 42 . Furthermore, HIF1α and HIF2α require specific oxygen levels for activation. Under 2–5% oxygen, HIF2α appears to be the dominant HIF, whereas HIF1α expression is increased with more critical hypoxic conditions, that is, less than 1% 43 . Previously, we have demonstrated that the HIF2α subunit co‐localizes with a fraction of AC133‐positive cells and that direct measurement of HIF2α expression correlates with CD133‐glycosylated upregulation and stem cell enrichment 17 ; however, it should be noted that levels of expression are not exclusive, that is, they do not overlap 24 , 44 . Griguer et al . demonstrated that the established glioblastoma cell line U251MG, previously recognized to be AC133‐negative, developed AC133‐positivity with decreasing oxygen tension 45 . Immunoreactivity of AC133, originally indistinguishable with normoxic culture conditions (21% oxygen), was detected in over 50% of the cell population following a 72 h incubation period with 1% oxygen. Subsequent reoxygenation of cells with 21% oxygen for 6 days restored the original low or background expression pattern. Observed plasticity of AC133 epitope expression correlated with mitochondrial functionality; inhibition of mitochondrial electron transport chain by Rotenone, a lipophilic compound and specific inhibitor of mitochondrial complex I, significantly increased AC133 expression in glioma cell lines, highlighting an inversely proportional relationship between mitochondrial membrane potential and AC133 epitope expression 45 , 46 . This demonstrates that loss in mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation plays a critical role in regulation of CD133 expression. These results suggest a revised version of tumour progression, placing strong emphasis on effects of the tumour microenvironment and induction of adaptive biological processes in tumour cells through genetic and mitochondrial mutations 47 . The model predicts that CSCs proliferate until they have produced nutritional and oxygen diffusion barriers 48 , resulting in severe growth limitations. A number of cells in close proximity to the most severe hypoxic levels experience significant loss of mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation and shift to glycolysis. This glycolytic shift initiates expression of stem cell markers, such as CD133. Population of cells at the margin of tumour invasion are re‐oxygenated and generate a new oxygen and nutrient diffusion barrier 45 . This model highlights the critical importance of metabolic flexibility for tumour progression in glioma 49 . One of the initial efforts to clarify stem cell dissemination in relation to hypoxic gradients in tissue, was identified by Chow et al . who established a mathematical framework to calculate partial pressure of oxygen (ppO 2 ) in the bone marrow haematopoietic compartment 50 , 51 , 52 . The haematopoietic stem cells were found to be localized in areas of very low ppO 2 , confirming a correlation between haematopoietic stem cell phenotype and regional hypoxia 53 . Specific regions of bone marrow containing low blood perfusions harboured the highest levels of haematopoietic stem cells with relation to oxygen perfusion gradient 53 . In a recent study, a tridimensional model on distribution of cellular and molecular phenotypic identities along the hypoxic gradient of GBM tumours was developed 50 . Three layers in the GBM mass were identified as core (necrotic) layer, intermediate layer, and peripheral layer. Using image‐guided surgery biopsies could be collected along the hypoxic gradient. Hypoxic markers such as HIF1α plus its downstream signalling targets CAIX and VEGF were seen to be more abundant in the inner, necrotic, low‐oxygenated portions of the GBM tumour mass, with dramatic reduction in expression in regions thought to be oxygenated. Furthermore, supporting the theory that hypoxia promotes a stem cell phenotype in brain tumours, immature cells expressing Nestin and CD133 were found to be localized within the central core and peripheral regions of the GBM mass 50 . Iida et al . recently identified that among the five promoters (P1–P5) of human CD133 gene loci in lung cancer cells, the P1 region was most strongly associated with hypoxia‐induced promoter activity 54 . P1 was identified to have direct binding sites of Oct4, which are essential for hypoxia‐induced promotion activity. The group confirmed direct binding of Oct4 transcription factor to the P1 promoter region of Prom1 resulting in upregulation of Prom1. In addition, enhanced expression of Prom1 was observed with direct binding of HIF2α to Oct4 54 – implicating an indirect upregulation of HIF2α with CD133. The microenvironment appears to have a significant impact on migratory and invasive propensities of stem cells. Recent experiments performed in our laboratories showed that levels of hypoxia (corresponding to 3% oxygen) resulted in increased AC133 expression, in paediatric glioblastoma (Fig. 3 ), coinciding with augmented proliferation. In addition, expression levels of prominent stem cell associated markers Nestin, Olig‐2, Musashi‐1 and Sox‐2 increased with reducing oxygen tensions 17 . In addition, McCord et al . have shown that stem cell‐like phenotypes were dependent on HIF2α transcription factor 55 . In agreement with this, we and others have documented that HIF2α is highly expressed in AC133‐positive cells, whereas HIF1α is expressed in AC133‐negative cells 17 , 43 . Immunohistological analysis of HIF2α and CD133 protein expression have shown co‐localization of antigens in close proximity to areas of necrosis and proliferating blood vessels. Furthermore, significant association between HIF2α transcription levels and poor patient prognosis has been demonstrated in glioblastoma 17 , 43 . Recent reports have identified molecular mechanisms in which HIFs directly modify cell differentiation and stem cell functions. Notch signalling is an attractive pathway for regulation of this process as it frequently functions to maintain NSC/progenitor states 56 , 57 . Gustafsson et al . have reported that hypoxia blocked NSC differentiation in a Notch‐dependent manner 57 . Notch signalling is activated by binding of Notch receptors to membrane‐bound ligands ‘Jagged’ and ‘Delta’, resulting in translocation of the Notch intracellular domain (ICD) to the nucleus, to form a DNA‐binding complex with co‐activators, such as p300, activating target gene expression 26 , 58 . It was also reported that hypoxia increased stabilization of Notch ICD and thus stimulation of Notch target genes, Hes‐1 and Hey‐2 , required to maintain proliferation potential of progenitor cells 59 , correlating with aforementioned HIF2α/CD133 associated expression and increased self‐renewal and survival. Upon translocation of HIF‐α and HIF‐β subunits to the cell nucleus, the subunits interact with hypoxia regulatory elements (HREs), recruiting transcriptional co‐activator p300, activating full transcription of downstream genes that regulate cell survival, motility, metabolism and angiogenesis. However, HIF subunits may themselves be recruited to the co‐activator to stabilize and potentiate expression of Notch target genes. It is therefore tempting to hypothesize that stem cells, residing in hypoxic niches, may recruit HIFs to activate and maintain Notch target gene expression, inhibiting differentiation, contributing to stem/progenitor cell self‐renewal 26 . Therefore, if increasing HIF2‐α transcript levels are maintaining Notch expression, it may be speculated that target genes of this pathway are responsible for increasing CD133 protein levels. Overexpression of oncogene ZNF217 has also been observed to contribute to maintenance of glioma stem cells regulated by HIFs. ZNF217 is frequently amplified in many types of tumour and is associated with aggressive tumour behaviour 60 . ZNF217 was recently shown to be amplified in 32% and overexpressed in 71.2% of GBM. ZNF217 expression was correlated with poor patient prognosis and it was more highly expressed in gliomas’ stem cells than in non glioma stem cells. Knockdown of either HIF1α or HIF2α inhibited ZNF217 expression, plus HIF2α knockdown significantly compromised upregulation of ZNF217 ; furthermore, increased expression of ZNF217 with 1% hypoxia correlated with upregulation of CD133 in glioma stem cells, demonstrating that ZNF217 contributes to maintenance of glioma CSCs under hypoxic conditions 60 . Figure 3. Open in a new tab Qualitative and quantitative characterisation of CD133 expression patterns under normoxic and hypoxic oxygen conditions: Characterisation of a paediatric glioblastoma with CD133/1 (AC133) (green fluorescence) and counterstained with the nuclear Hoechst stain (blue fluorescence) under normoxic (a) and hypoxic (b) culture conditions with fluorescent immunocytochemistry. Scale bar = 32.00 μm. Decreasing oxygen tensions significantly increased the percentage mean (% gated expression) of CD133 when further analysed with flow cytometry (c). A Student''s t ‐test was used for statistical evaluation ( P < 0.005). All data presented are representative of three independent experiments performed in triplicate ( n = 3). All values are shown as arithmetic means ± SEM. It is well recognized that glioma cells may either divide, or transiently arrest from the cell cycle and invade 61 , suggesting that rate of cell proliferation is inversely proportional to invasion 62 . Compliant with this theory, recent reports have identified AC133‐negative cells demonstrating an invasive phenotype in glioma cells 17 , 18 . Oxygen tension also stimulates tumour growth and metastasis via induction of angiogenesis, through release of VEGF 63 , 64 . In this content, Zagzag et al . demonstrated that HIF1‐α and VEGF, (a highly potent cytokine known to promote angiogenesis), co‐localize in GBM cells for VEGF‐mediated angiogenesis 65 ; therefore, it is likely that hypoxia induces HIF1α expression in GBMs, which subsequently activates VEGF transcription, increasing VEGF activity and promoting angiogenesis. Another gene hypothesized to be positively regulated by HIF1α is CXCR4 , a chemokine receptor for stromal cell‐derived 1α, and a crucial molecule associated with tumour progression and metastatic spread 66 , 67 , 68 , 69 , 70 . Several studies have investigated the functional role of CXCR4 in glioma 71 , 72 , 73 , 74 , 75 . For example, CXCR4 has been shown to facilitate a critical role in glioma cell invasion. CXCR4 levels have been shown to correlate with expression levels of HIF1α in human GBM samples and to be expressed in angiogenic tumour vessels in vivo . CXCR4 expression has been observed to be significantly induced in glioma cells following exposure to hypoxia in vitro . Glioma cells infected with lentivirus‐mediated shRNA directed against HIF1α did not express HIF1α or CXCR4 following exposure to hypoxia in vitro . Furthermore, migration studies under hypoxic conditions with AMD3100, an inhibitor of CXCR4, have shown a significant decrease in migratory rates of glioma cells 65 . This demonstrates not only the importance of CXCR4 in glioma invasion but also significance of HIF1α in tumour cell invasion. Upregulation of HIF1α transcript expression may therefore induce the invasive phenotype of AC133‐negative cells. From the aforementioned reports, it appears that CD133/AC133 expression is maintained in hypoxic niches that are critical targets for tumour therapies 76 , 77 . Glycosylation of CD133 There is increasing evidence for an association between glycosylated CD133‐expression and identification of stem and progenitor cells, in a variety tissues of the human body, including endothelial progenitor cells, myogenic cells and prostatic epithelial stem cells 78 , 79 , 80 . Ono and Hakomori have suggested that degree of tumour malignancy could be controlled by many factors, for example, (i) invasion; (ii) adhesion; (iii) matrix‐degradation; (iv) angiogenesis; and (v) apoptosis. Mechanisms of function each of these is maintained either directly or indirectly by N‐ or O‐linked glycosylation, or by surrounding gangliosides 81 . Expression of CD133 in cells of colon cancer cell lines (HT‐29‐D4 and Caco‐2) has been studied by generating a new anti‐CD133 antibody using an immunogenic synthetic peptide developed from the N‐terminal extracellular domain of CD133 82 . This group demonstrated that CD133 expression reduced on cell differentiation, a finding which is consistent with research conducted by Kemper et al . 82 , 83 . One of the major results of this work was that the immunogenic synthetic peptide displayed increased affinity for gangliosides, such as GD3. It was also demonstrated that GD3 could inhibit binding of anti‐CD133 immunogenic synthetic peptide. Inhibition of this binding domain with exogenous micellar gangliosides (GM1 and GM3) did not affect binding of the AC133 antibody to the glycoprotein, suggesting that glycosylation sites are present on the second and third extracellular domains of the protein 83 . It has been reported that functions of integrins are, usually, controlled by N ‐glycosylation. De‐ N ‐glycosylation of α v β 1 results in dissociation of the α v β 1 subunits and loss of cellular binding to fibronectin 84 . Gangliosides have been shown to affect integrin receptor function; for example, binding of fibronectin by α v β 1 was strongly enhanced at specific concentrations of ganglioside GM3, whereas other gangliosides, such as GD2 and GD3, did not affect binding propensity 85 . Hakomori et al . also demonstrated that CD133 can be saturated by gangliosides shed by tumour cells, which affect stem cell phenotype of CD133 84 . Amounts of GD3 in cell membranes at any one time, may determine biological behaviour as GD3 is constantly being formed in the rough endoplasmic reticulum, is shunted to the cell surface or is endocytosed. In contrast, it may be that the glycosylation of the CD133 protein controls the biological behaviour of GD3. Furthermore, the group hypothesized that CD133 may facilitate an active role in recruitment of CSCs, suggesting that the glycoprotein may function as a signalling unit to control tumour cell phenotype 84 . Alternatively, glycosylated CD133 may sequester GD3 antigen to the N‐terminal of the protein. Binding of the ganglioside may cause extracellular sequestration of endoglycosidases, oligosaccharide‐cleaving enzymes, resulting in de‐ N ‐glycosylation of the CD133 molecule, and switching of integrin receptors, promoting the invasive phenotype of CD133−ve cells. Ohkawa et al . demonstrated that GD3+ve cells treated with siRNA of integrin β1, displayed markedly suppressed proliferation and invasion, compared to control cells 86 . These results suggest that GD3 may be implicated in upregulation of invasion and proliferation through enhancement of integrin signalling 87 . The group further reported that GD3 enhances integrin‐mediated signalling in malignant melanoma, leading to increased cell proliferation, invasion and adhesion signalling 87 . A further alternative is that although there may be a plausible ganglioside binding site at the N‐terminal of the CD133 molecule, binding of GD3 may be unrelated to invasion propensity of CD133−ve cells. In contrast, binding may directly influence invasion of CD133+ve cells. Taïeb et al . have demonstrated that ganglioside binding does not affect CD133 glycosylation; it may be possible that the ganglioside inhibits extracellular N‐terminal of the CD133 molecule, facilitating, in part, the increased invasive propensity of AC133‐negative cells 82 . Reports on CD133 expression differ between studies, varying from mRNA expression and protein levels to glycosylation of CD133. CD133 protein has eight possible glycosylation regions on its two extracellular loop domains permitting differential glycosylation, highlighting the impact of differential glycosylation on the identification process of CD133 9 , and further supported by the finding that upon differentiation, expression of glycosylation‐reliant AC133 epitope, is downregulated in a manner that positively correlated with loss of clonogenicity 6 , 9 . Upon differentiation, alteration in CD133 promoter activity is not observed 83 ; it therefore appears that differential glycosylation results in distinct overall of its tertiary structure, differential folding of the protein or localization of CD133 on the membrane that prohibits antibodies’ access to their epitopes, masking epitopes of CD133 protein. Furthermore, transcription of CD133 may be initiated at different restricted promoters, resulting in numerous alternatively spliced transcripts (Fig. 4 ) 9 . Ardebili et al . confirmed that survival rate of GBM patients with higher CD133 mRNA expression was significantly shorter than those patients with lower CD133 mRNA levels 18 . AC133 immunoreactivity appears to be restricted to undifferentiated cells of both adult and embryonic tissues 6 , 9 ; in contrast, CD133 protein has been identified in a variety of tissues, including kidney, uterus, human eye and sweat glands 83 , 84 , 85 , 86 , 87 , 88 , 89 , 90 , 91 . It has been theorized that post‐translational modification of CD133 may control AC133 surface recognition. Mak et al . recently showed that loss of complex N ‐glycosylation does not modify stability nor transmembrane location of CD133, supporting the hypothesis of Kemper et al . that differential N ‐glycan modifications play a role in stabilization of a specific CD133 conformation, or alter tertiary structure of CD133, to expose the AC133 epitope. 92 . Furthermore, MGAT4C, a glycosyltransferase involved in catalysing GlcNAcβ1–4 linkage to mannose residues of glycans, has been identified to complex directly with CD133 N ‐glycans 92 , highlighting a role for CD133 as an MGAT4C substrate for N ‐glycan processing, contributing to cell surface recognition of AC133. Figure 4. Open in a new tab Schematic representation of the CD133 mRNA transcript, indicating the regions of N‐linked glycosylation. Downregulation of CD133 by short‐hairpin RNA (shRNA) in metastatic melanoma cells has been demonstrated to impair cell population growth and migration and to reduce formation of spheroids under defined stem cell conditions 93 . Downregulation of CD133 has been observed to upregulate expression of specific Wnt pathway inhibitors, identifying a link between CD133 and the Wnt signalling pathway; however, whether CD133 co‐operates directly or indirectly with Wnt remains unresolved 93 . Little is known regarding functionality of the C‐terminal region of CD133 protein. Boivin et al . showed that it is phosphorylated at the juxtamembrane tyrosine‐828, located within the YDDV motif, which, upon phosphorylation, serves as a binding site for SH2 domain cytoplasmic targets Src, Fyn and Nck, converting the C‐terminal domain of CD133 into a signalling unit 3 . It is therefore tempting to hypothesize that tyrosine phosphorylation of CD133 protein in response to extracellular ligands may stimulate intracellular signalling pathways, such as Wnt, influencing cell population growth, proliferation, migration and metastatic potential. Although these pieces of evidence suggests that CD133 expression is not restricted to CSC, it does imply that glycosylation supports the hierarchical model for tumour initiation. Furthermore, given the molecular and cellular heterogeneity of gliomas, it is likely that these tumours are composed of both AC133‐dependent and AC133‐independent CSCs 42 . The vast majority of in vitro examinations of CD133+ve cells have been performed with the glycosylation‐dependent epitope AC133; little glioma analysis employs sole use of CD133 protein expression for isolation and characterization of CSCs 16 . Indeed, it may be that glycosylation status of CD133 is a more specific marker of the CSC phenotype than CD133 protein levels. Conclusion Despite the ongoing controversy surrounding CD133 as a definitive cancer stem cell marker, no one can deny the continual accumulation of data correlating AC133 expression with poor patient prognosis 13 , and highlighting it as a potential prognostic significance. Because of anti‐CD133 antibodies used to identify indeterminate glycosylated epitopes, glycosylation status rather than protein or mRNA expression of CD133 may act as a more precise marker of the CSC phenotype. Cancer stem cells have been reported to be the only tumourigenic population in GBM, maintaining tumour growth and development due to an unlimited proliferation potential, being inherently radio‐ and chemotherapy‐resistant, and maybe residing in vivo in a niche characterized by reduced oxygen tension 94 , 95 . Therefore, these cells represent an attractive therapeutic target for total eradication of a tumour mass. If GBMs are indeed maintained by CSCs, cells that are characterized by reduced rates of proliferation and division, it is only logical that radio‐ and chemotherapies, which exclusively target actively cycling cells, are likely to be ineffective. Summarizing the aforementioned findings, AC133 expression may be consistent with locations of hypoxia within tumours, thus serving as explanation for increased radioresistance observed in tumours with high AC133 expression. Challenging fundamental roles of hypoxia, mitochondrial functionality and HIFs may provide further insight into the complex regulation of CD133, and suggest/provide innovative therapeutic targets in glioma therapy. Holy grail it may not be, but CD133 may constitute a potential candidate for modulation of biological behaviour of primary brain tumours, a candidature which remains difficult to ascertain due to its oxygen‐, glycosylation‐related promiscuity of expression. Directly targeting the CD133+ve population is an attractive approach, which may not only eradicate much tumourigenic behaviour associated with these cells, but may also render them more susceptible to downstream‐targeted therapies. Acknowledgements The authors would like to thank Ali''s Dream, Charlie''s Challenge, Brain Tumour Research, and the Isle of Man Anticancer Association, for their generous support. References 1. Corbeil D, R