INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(341,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10712979','Predictive Value of the International Classification of Diseases, 9th Revision Codes for Identifying Ocular Oncology Diagnoses.','Wang KY, Xu TT, White LJ, Dalvin LA.','2023','olmsted ppvs npvs dermoid olmsted olmsted olmsted olmsted olmsted olmsted olmsted olmsted olmsted incongruent incongruent chrpe dermoid hidrocystoma neurofibroma complexion melanosis melanosis olmsted olmsted chrpe eyeball dermoid canthus canthus canthus hidrocystoma canthus neurofibroma canthus canthus canthus canthus complexion melanosis pigmentations melanosis pigmentations ppvs dermoid npvs chrpe hidrocystoma neurofibroma dermoid chrpe dermoid hidrocystoma neurofibroma complexion melanosis melanosis olmsted ppvs eyeball eyeball hidrocystoma neurofibroma canthus canthus uner villalobos hamedani nonstandardized midwestern olmsted olmsted r01ag034676 hoeft ca015083 ctsa kl2 tr002379 launia dalvin r01ag034676 hoeft ca015083 ctsa kl2 tr002379 tamboli topham zabor radivoyevitch kilic jemm kalirai pimentel janardhana borkar ophthalmicus episcleritis uchiyama faez nasir unizony plenge papaliodis jabs bothun hendricks mansukhani bothun hamedani lott deveney muro villarreal virgili gatta ciccolallo capocaccia biggeri crocetti bhatti sobti chadha cauchi melton yawn sauver grossardt melton rochesterproject rochesterproject alibhai dually topaz shafran topaz bowles uner aronow mruthyunjaya materin villalobos byfield ghate adejoro dieleman mccracken caraballo ndumele herrin e223856','Abstract Introduction The aim of this study was to determine the predictive value of International Classification of Diseases, 9th Revision (ICD-9) billing codes for identifying ocular oncology diagnoses. Methods Population-based retrospective cohort study of all Olmsted County, Minnesota residents with any ocular neoplasm-related ICD-9 code from January 1, 2006 to October 1, 2015. All medical records were reviewed for confirmation of ocular neoplasm. Diagnoses with ≥5 cases confirmed via a medical record review were compared to corresponding ICD-9 codes. Main outcome measures included positive predictive value (PPV), negative predictive value (NPV), sensitivity, and specificity of ICD-9 codes. Results Among 3,932 subjects with ≥1 ocular neoplasm-related ICD-9 code, 21 diagnoses met study criteria. The most frequent intraocular, extraocular/orbital, and ocular surface diagnoses were choroidal nevus ( n = 824), epidermal inclusion cyst ( n = 263), and conjunctival nevus ( n = 74), respectively. PPVs ranged from 1.2% to 73.8%, NPVs from 96.9% to 100%, sensitivity from 0% to 100%, and specificity from 85.7% to 100%. Among malignant neoplasms, PPV ranged from 0% to 73.8%: ocular surface squamous neoplasia (PPV: 0%), choroidal melanoma (PPV: 25.0%), eyelid squamous cell carcinoma (PPV: 46.7%), and eyelid basal cell carcinoma (PPV: 73.8%). Among benign neoplasms, PPV ranged from 1.2% (dermoid cyst) to 61.6% (choroidal nevus). Conclusion There was a wide variation in a predictive value of ocular neoplasm-related ICD-9 billing codes, which suggests that ocular oncology-related claims data alone may overestimate the true number of ocular oncology diagnoses. Keywords: Ocular oncology; Claims data; International Classification of Diseases, 9th Revision; International Classification of Diseases for Oncology; ICD-9; ICD-O; Positive predictive value; Negative predictive value; Sensitivity; Specificity Introduction Administrative claims in clinical registries and database studies have been increasingly used in ophthalmology research, including ocular oncology [ 1 , 2 ]. While claims data have the benefit of creating large sample sizes for investigation of rare conditions, the variable accuracy of billing codes in identifying diagnoses remains a significant limitation [ 3 , 4 ]. Therefore, it is important to understand the predictive value of claims data to accurately interpret the validity of studies that utilize billing codes to identify diagnoses. Prior studies of International Classification of Diseases (ICD) billing code accuracy have shown that common conditions (e.g., cataract, diabetic retinopathy, and age-related macular degeneration) tend to have a greater predictive value [ 5 ]. In contrast, ICD codes for less common diagnoses (e.g., neuro-ophthalmic, ocular inflammatory, pediatric eye diagnoses) may be less accurate [ 6 – 9 ]. Given the rarity of many ocular neoplasms, clinical registries and database studies are valuable data sources in ocular oncology. Clinical registries and claims data have been previously utilized in the ocular oncology literature [ 10 – 12 ]; however, no studies to date have assessed the accuracy of claims data for capturing ocular neoplasms to the best of our knowledge. The purpose of this study was to investigate the predictive value of ICD-9 codes for identifying ocular oncology diagnoses. Methods This investigation was a secondary analysis of a larger study investigating ocular oncology epidemiology in Olmsted County, Minnesota. The medical records of all patients of any age residing in Olmsted County, Minnesota diagnosed with any ocular neoplasm from January 1, 2006 to October 1, 2015 were retrospectively reviewed using the Rochester Epidemiology Project (REP). The REP is a medical record linkage system that tracks medical care delivered to over 150,000 unique residents of Olmsted County, Minnesota using diagnostic and surgical procedure codes [ 13 ]. Given that Olmsted County is relatively isolated from other urban areas and that the REP tracks care delivered across Mayo Clinic, Olmsted Medical Group, and small independent clinics, the REP captures virtually all medical care delivered to the residents of Olmsted County, Minnesota [ 14 ]. Sex- and race-specific mid-study population counts were obtained from the REP Census of Olmsted County for 2010 [ 15 ]. The mid-study point was January 1, 2010. The Institutional Review Boards of Mayo Clinic and Olmsted Medical Center approved this study. An extensive diagnostic code search utilizing 45 ocular neoplasm-related ICD-9 codes was performed to identify all potential patients diagnosed with any ocular neoplasm. This study analyzed ICD-9 codes only and did not review the accuracy of ICD-10 codes, which were implemented by our institution on October 2, 2015. Patients were excluded if they were diagnosed outside the study period or if they resided outside Olmsted County at the time of diagnosis. All medical records identified via the diagnostic code search were retrospectively reviewed to assess diagnoses and demographic data. The number of potential cases identified via initial ICD-9 code search was documented for each diagnosis. After the review of the medical records, the number of cases identified via ICD-9 code search versus medical record-confirmed diagnoses was compared. The accurate diagnosis was defined as the diagnosis confirmed by the manual review of the medical record based on clinical diagnosis, exam, and/or histopathologic confirmation when available. Cases were categorized into 4 categories: present in both medical record and ICD-9 code search ( true positives ), absent in both medical record and ICD-9 code search ( true negatives ), present in medical record and absent in ICD-9 code search ( false negatives ), and absent in medical record and present in ICD-9 code search ( false positives ). Diagnoses that had ≥5 confirmed cases via a review of the medical record during the nearly 10-year period were evaluated for ICD-9 code validity ( Table 1 ). We selected ≥5 cases as the inclusion criteria to include as many diagnoses as possible while avoiding reporting potentially unreliable results for diagnoses with very small case numbers; a higher cutoff point would have missed clinically important lesions that are less prevalent such as choroidal melanoma, particularly given our population-based design rather than referral-based study. Diagnoses identified via ICD-9 codes alone were compared to diagnoses confirmed via an individual review of the medical record. If the correct diagnosis was present in the ICD-9 code search and the review of the medical record, or when the diagnosis was absent in both ICD-9 code search and medical record, the ICD-9 code accuracy was considered congruent. If the diagnoses were reflected in the ICD-9 coding but not in the medical record, or vice versa, the ICD-9 code accuracy was considered incongruent. Table 1. Number of cases identified via a review of the medical record versus Rochester Epidemiology Project diagnostic code search Diagnosis Number of potential cases identified via initial ICD-9 code search Number of cases confirmed via a manual review of medical record Histopathology available, N , (%) Medical record data versus ICD-9 code search data congruent incongruent present in both medical record and ICD-9 code search (true positives) absent in both medical record and ICD-9 code search (true negatives) present in medical record, and absent in ICD-9 code search (false negatives) absent in medical record, and present in ICD-9 code search (false positives) Intraocular Choroidal melanoma 24 9 2 (22.2) 6 3,905 3 18 Choroidal nevus 1,153 824 0 (0.0) 710 2,665 114 443 Congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium (CHRPE) 0 6 0 (0.0) 0 3,926 6 0 Iris nevus 0 76 1 (1.3) 0 3,856 76 0 Extraocular/orbital Dermoid cyst 485 12 5 (41.7) 6 3,441 6 479 Ductal epithelial cyst lacrimal gland 4 9 5 (55.6) 1 3,920 8 3 Epidermal inclusion cyst 485 4 0 (0.0) 250 3,434 13 235 Eyelid actinic keratosis 0 8 7 (87.5) 0 3,924 8 0 Eyelid basal cell carcinoma 84 181 172 (95.6) 62 3,729 119 22 Eyelid cutaneous nevus 0 8 5 (62.5) 0 3,924 8 0 Eyelid hidrocystoma 0 59 43 (72.9) 0 3,873 59 0 Eyelid neurofibroma 0 5 4 (80.0) 0 3,927 5 0 Eyelid papilloma 0 25 7 (28.0) 0 3,907 25 0 Eyelid sebaceous cyst 485 40 7 (17.5) 40 3,447 0 445 Eyelid seborrheic keratosis 0 13 10 (83.3) 0 3,920 12 0 Eyelid squamous cell carcinoma 15 47 45 (95.7) 7 3,877 40 8 Ocular surface Conjunctival complexion-associated melanosis 104 16 0 (0.0) 6 3,818 10 98 Conjunctival cyst 219 5 1 (20.0) 0 3,708 5 219 Conjunctival nevus 97 75 16 (21.3) 36 3,797 38 61 Ocular surface squamous neoplasia 13 6 3 (50.0) 0 3,913 6 13 Primary acquired melanosis 104 62 11 (17.7) 38 3,804 24 66 Open in a new tab N , number of cases confirmed via an individual review of the medical record. The main outcome measures were positive predictive value (PPV) and negative predictive value (NPV) of ICD-9 codes for each diagnosis. PPV was defined as the ratio of the number of confirmed cases by the review of the medical record with an accurate ICD-9 code ( true positives ) to the number of medical records identified via ICD-9 code search alone ( true positives plus false positives ). NPV was defined as the ratio of the number of cases without a medical record-confirmed diagnosis or the associated ICD-9 code ( true negatives ) to the number of all cases that did not hold an ICD-9 code for a particular diagnosis ( true negatives plus false negatives ). Sensitivity and specificity were also calculated as secondary outcome measures for each diagnosis. Data analysis was performed using SAS Version 9 (SAS Institute; Cary, NC, USA). Results The mid-study population of Olmsted County, Minnesota in 2010 was 148,873. The initial ocular neoplasm-related ICD code search identified 3,932 subjects. After the review of individual medical records, 1,852 (47.1%) subjects were confirmed to have an ocular neoplasm. Among the 1,852 subjects with an ocular neoplasm, 1,017 (54.9%) were female and 1,685 (91.0%) were White, comparable to the demographic characteristics of the overall Olmsted County mid-study (2010) population, which was 52.3% female and 81.8% White [ 15 ]. Overall, 21 diagnoses had ≥5 cases confirmed via a review of the medical records and therefore met study criteria: 4 diagnoses were intraocular, 12 were extraocular/orbital, and 5 were ocular surface. These 21 diagnoses and their corresponding ICD-9 codes considered accurate are listed in Table 2 . The most frequent intraocular, extraocular/orbital, and ocular surface diagnoses were choroidal nevus ( N = 824), epidermal inclusion cyst ( N = 263), and conjunctival nevus ( N = 74), respectively. Table 2. International Classification of Diseases, 9th Revision (ICD-9) codes with corresponding diagnosis Diagnosis Corresponding ICD-9 code ICD-9 code description Intraocular Choroidal melanoma 190.6 Malignant neoplasm of choroid Choroidal nevus 224.6 Benign neoplasm of choroid Congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium (CHRPE) 362.76 Dystrophies primarily involving the retinal pigment epithelium Iris nevus 224.0, 364.89 Benign neoplasm of eyeball, except conjunctiva, cornea, retina, and choroid (224.0) Other disorders of iris and ciliary body (364.89) Extraocular/orbital Dermoid cyst 374.84 Cysts of eyelids Ductal epithelial cyst lacrimal gland 224.2 Benign neoplasm of lacrimal gland Epidermal inclusion cyst 374.84 Cysts of eyelids Eyelid actinic keratosis 216.1 Benign neoplasm of eyelid, including canthus Eyelid basal cell carcinoma 173.11 Basal cell carcinoma of eyelid, including canthus Eyelid cutaneous nevus 216.1 Benign neoplasm of eyelid, including canthus Eyelid hidrocystoma 216.1 Benign neoplasm of eyelid, including canthus Eyelid neurofibroma 216.1 Benign neoplasm of eyelid, including canthus Eyelid papilloma 216.1 Benign neoplasm of eyelid, including canthus Eyelid sebaceous cyst 374.84 Cysts of eyelids Eyelid seborrheic keratosis 216.1 Benign neoplasm of eyelid, including canthus Eyelid squamous cell carcinoma 173.12 Squamous cell carcinoma of eyelid, including canthus Ocular surface Conjunctival complexion-associated melanosis 372.55, 372.56 Conjunctival pigmentations (372.55) Conjunctival deposits (372.56) Conjunctival cyst 372.75 Conjunctival cysts Conjunctival nevus 224.3 Benign neoplasm of conjunctiva Ocular surface squamous neoplasia 190.3, 190.4 Malignant neoplasm of conjunctiva (190.3) Malignant neoplasm of cornea (190.4) Primary acquired melanosis 372.55, 372.56 Conjunctival pigmentations (372.55) Conjunctival deposits (372.56) Open in a new tab The number of cases identified via ICD-9 codes alone versus the number of medical record-confirmed cases is reported in Table 1 . For the 21 diagnoses in the 3,932 medical records with ≥1 eye-related ICD code, PPVs ranged from 1.2% (dermoid cyst) to 73.8% (eyelid basal cell carcinoma) ( Table 3 ). The NPVs ranged from 96.9% (eyelid basal cell carcinoma) to 100% (eyelid sebaceous cyst). Sensitivity ranged from 0% (conjunctival cyst) to 100% (eyelid sebaceous cyst). Specificity ranged from 85.7% (choroidal nevus) to 100% (CHRPE, iris nevus, eyelid actinic keratosis, eyelid cutaneous nevus, eyelid hidrocystoma, eyelid neurofibroma, eyelid papilloma, eyelid seborrheic keratosis). Among benign neoplasms, PPV ranged from 1.2% (dermoid cyst) to 61.6% (choroidal nevus). Among malignant neoplasms, PPV ranged from 0% to 73.8%: ocular surface squamous neoplasia (PPV: 0%), choroidal melanoma (PPV: 25.0%), eyelid squamous cell carcinoma (PPV: 46.7%), and eyelid basal cell carcinoma (PPV: 73.8%). Predictive values, sensitivity, and specificity of the ICD-9 codes for each diagnosis are listed in Table 3 . Table 3. Predictive values, sensitivity, and specificity of ICD-9 codes for capturing ocular neoplasm diagnoses Diagnosis PPV NPV Sensitivity Specificity Intraocular, n (%) Choroidal melanoma 25.0 99.9 66.7 99.5 Choroidal nevus 61.6 95.9 86.2 85.7 Congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium (CHRPE) – 99.8 0.0 100.0 Iris nevus – 98.1 0.0 100.0 Extraocular/orbital, n (%) Dermoid cyst 1.2 99.8 50.0 87.8 Ductal epithelial cyst lacrimal gland 25.0 99.8 11.1 99.9 Epidermal inclusion cyst 51.5 99.6 95.1 93.6 Eyelid actinic keratosis – 99.8 0.0 100.0 Eyelid basal cell carcinoma 73.8 96.9 34.3 99.4 Eyelid cutaneous nevus – 99.8 0.0 100.0 Eyelid hidrocystoma – 98.5 0.0 100.0 Eyelid neurofibroma – 99.9 0.0 100.0 Eyelid papilloma – 99.4 0.0 100.0 Eyelid sebaceous cyst 8.2 100.0 100.0 88.6 Eyelid seborrheic keratosis – 99.7 0.0 100.0 Eyelid squamous cell carcinoma 46.7 99.0 14.9 99.8 Ocular surface, n (%) Conjunctival complexion-associated melanosis 5.8 99.7 37.5 97.5 Conjunctival cyst 0.0 99.9 0.0 94.4 Conjunctival nevus 37.1 99.0 48.6 98.4 Ocular surface squamous neoplasia 0.0 99.8 0.0 99.7 Primary acquired melanosis 36.5 99.4 61.3 98.3 Open in a new tab N , number of cases confirmed via an individual review of the medical record; ICD-9, International Classification of Diseases, 9th Revision. Discussion In this population-based cohort of 3,932 residents from Olmsted County, Minnesota identified via an extensive search of diagnostic claims data for all ocular neoplasms, PPVs of ICD-9 codes for ocular neoplasms ranged widely from 1.2% to 73.8%. Among benign lesions, PPV varied from 1.2% to 61.6%. Similarly for malignant lesions, PPV ranged widely from 0% to 73.8%. These findings suggest that the predictive value of ICD-9 billing codes for capturing ocular neoplasms is widely variable. The variable accuracy of ocular neoplasm-related ICD-9 codes was likely related to the accuracy of ICD-9 code descriptions for each diagnosis. Diagnoses with vague or nonspecific ICD-9 code descriptions tended toward weaker claims data predictive value. For example, there was no ICD-9 code explicitly describing iris nevus, which had a sensitivity of 0% and a specificity of 100% in this study. We therefore had to utilize nonspecific diagnostic codes 224.0 (benign neoplasm of eyeball, except conjunctiva, cornea, retina, and choroid) and 364.89 (other disorders of iris and ciliary body) to identify iris nevus diagnoses. PPV for iris nevus could not be calculated because PPV was defined as the ratio of true positives to true positives plus false positives, and there were no iris nevus cases that were considered true positive or false positive. Thus, ICD-9 codes with nonspecific descriptions such as 224.0 (benign neoplasm of eyeball, except conjunctiva, cornea, retina, and choroid) or 364.89 (other disorders of iris and ciliary body) were considered accurate for a diagnosis of iris nevus, but these nonspecific billing codes could also be used to capture several other findings such as iris cyst or iris nodule. Similarly, no specific ICD-9 codes existed for multiple benign eyelid lesions including actinic keratosis (sensitivity: 0%), cutaneous nevus (sensitivity: 0%), hidrocystoma (sensitivity: 0%), neurofibroma (sensitivity: 0%), papilloma (sensitivity: 0%), and seborrheic keratosis (sensitivity: 0%). ICD-9 code 216.1 (other benign neoplasm of skin of unspecified eyelid, including canthus) was considered the accurate billing code for all these lesions and resulted in poor sensitivity for identifying these eyelid lesions since this billing code was relatively nonspecific. In contrast, diagnoses associated with explicitly defined ICD-9 code descriptions tended toward greater accuracy, such as 224.6 (benign neoplasm of choroid) for choroidal nevus (PPV: 61.6%) and 173.11 (basal cell carcinoma of skin of unspecific eyelid, including canthus) for eyelid basal cell carcinoma (PPV: 73.8%). The lack of ICD-9 claims data that explicitly define ocular oncology conditions represents a major inherent limitation of claims data for identifying ocular oncology diagnoses in database studies utilizing ICD-9 codes to identify cases. The variable predictive value of claims data in ocular oncology has implications on prior studies that utilized ICD-9 claims data to identify diagnoses. Approximately 7,000 cases of uveal melanoma were identified in European cancer registries using International Classification of Diseases for Oncology (ICD-O) and ICD-9 codes; specifically 4,665 subjects were identified as having either retinal or choroidal melanoma if they had the ICD-9 code 190.5 (malignant neoplasm of retina) or 190.6 (malignant neoplasm of choroid), respectively [ 10 ]. Similarly, a nationwide Taiwanese cohort of 889 primary eye cancers over a 17-year span was identified using ICD-9 190 codes [ 11 ], and a cohort of 220 patients with uveal melanoma in Scotland was identified using ICD-9 and ICD-10 codes [ 12 ]. In this study, the PPV of ICD-9 code 190.6 for identifying choroidal melanoma was 25%, suggesting that the use of this ICD-9 billing code to identify choroidal melanoma may overestimate the true number of cases. An important consideration of our study was that there were only 9 cases of choroidal melanoma, thus limiting our ability to assess ICD-9 predictive value. We were also unable to investigate ICD-O code accuracy since our study design utilized the REP, which uses population-based US county level data as opposed to cancer registry data. Lastly, we were unable to comment on ICD-9 accuracy of these diagnoses due to insufficient number of iris melanoma and ciliary body melanoma cases. ICD-10 codes were not assessed due to inherent limitations of our study period. It is possible that ICD-10 codes could generate higher quality data due to their greater diagnostic code specificity [ 16 ]; however, several initial studies have suggested that ICD-9 and ICD-10 codes have similar validity and ICD-10 administration has not significantly improved administrative data quality [ 17 , 18 ]. In a pilot analysis using the IRIS Registry to determine the feasibility of using ICD-9 and ICD-10 data to study uveal melanoma, Uner and colleagues reported the need for research to rely on expanded disease-specific data collection as data elements of interest are often incomplete due to limitations in diagnostic code specificity. Based on their pilot analysis of uveal melanoma, lack of procedure codes and tumor-specific information (e.g., tumor location/size, pathological confirmation, distant metastases, mortality) pose a challenge to investigating uveal melanoma using big data [ 19 ]. A strategy to mitigate the limitations of ICD-9 code accuracy could include incorporating complementary search criteria to diagnosis codes. Some examples of complementary criteria are limiting diagnoses to only those made by appropriate specialists or those associated with appropriate diagnostic workup and/or treatment. In a study evaluating treatment therapies for soft tissue carcinoma, Villalobos and colleagues integrated drugs known to treat sarcoma (e.g., docetaxel, gemcitabine, doxorubicin) into their search criteria to identify patients and reduce bias [ 20 ]. Similarly, in a systematic review of ICD codes in classifying neuro-ophthalmic disease, Hamedani and colleagues [ 8 ] suggested that ICD code accuracy may also improve when solely using diagnoses made by neurologists and ophthalmologists. Ultimately, ocular oncology and other rare ophthalmic disease studies that utilize ICD-9 or ICD-10 codes likely need complementary data to improve their validity. Limitations of this study included its retrospective design, which contributed to nonstandardized diagnostic criteria, examination, workup, data collection, and follow-up. As a result, diagnoses may have been omitted in the medical record, missed, or erroneous (i.e., misdiagnosis). Furthermore, patients who may have had an ocular neoplasm diagnosis without an associated diagnostic code could have been missed in this study, but this is unlikely, given that we searched a comprehensive list of 45 ICD codes. This population-based study design also favored common benign lesions over rarer but clinically relevant lesions. For example, the present study had no cases of retinoblastoma and 9 cases of choroidal melanoma. We mitigated exclusion of rare but clinically relevant lesions by setting a low threshold of ≥5 cases as inclusion criteria. While these inclusion criteria may have reduced the number of diagnoses in this study, the utility of analyzing ICD-9 coding accuracy for diagnoses with <5 cases would be limited due to low statistical power. We recognize that certain benign lesions may have been excluded if there were fewer than 5 diagnosed cases due to the asymptomatic nature of many lesions and potential lack of diagnostic eye exam. However, only one diagnostic visit was required to contribute to the number of cases for this study. Furthermore, as ocular conditions were assessed among subjects in a predominately White Midwestern US county population, lack of racial and/or ethnic diversity could limit the generalizability of these findings to areas with a different population mix. Lesions such as choroidal nevus might be more noticeable in White individuals with a lighter colored fundus, and social demographic factors could affect access to care between racial groups [ 21 , 22 ]. In addition, our study period of January 1, 2006 to October 1, 2015 had limited generalizability since ICD-10 codes were implemented on October 2, 2015; therefore, we only analyzed ICD-9 codes. Similarly, our findings might not apply to cancer registry-based studies that utilized ICD-O codes; further studies are needed to evaluate a predictive value of ICD-10 and ICD-O diagnostic codes in ocular oncology. Lastly, we acknowledge that the predictive values of ICD-9 codes in this study were highly dependent on the eye care providers assigning the billing codes and could vary between practices, institutions, and health care systems. Therefore, the external generalizability of our findings may be limited. In summary, claims data demonstrated widely variable PPV from 1.2% to 73.8% for ocular neoplasms in this cohort of 3,932 patients with at least 1 ocular oncology-related ICD-9 code. This trend was observed in both benign lesions, varying from 1.2% to 61.6%, and malignant lesions, ranging from 0% to 73.8%. These findings suggest that studies utilizing claims data might overestimate the true number of diagnoses, and ocular oncology studies that solely use claims data to identify cases should be interpreted with caution given the varying predictive values of ICD-9 codes. Statement of Ethics This study complied with the tenets of the Declaration of Helsinki, all federal and state laws in Minnesota, and the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act. This study protocol was reviewed and approved by the Institutional Review Boards of Mayo Clinic and Olmsted Medical Center (IRB Number 21-001266). Consent to participate statement: This study was retrospective and considered minimal risk; therefore, no additional informed consent from patients was required by the Institutional Review Boards of Mayo Clinic and Olmsted Medical Center, and waiver of consent was granted. Conflict of Interest Statement The authors have no conflicts of interest to declare. Funding Sources This study was made possible using the resources of the Rochester Epidemiology Project, which is supported by the National Institute on Aging of the National Institutes of Health under Award Number R01AG034676. This publication was made possible through the support of the Leonard and Mary Lou Hoeft Career Development Award Fund in Ophthalmology Research, Grant No. P30 CA015083 from the National Cancer Institute, and CTSA Grant No. KL2 TR002379 from the National Center for Advancing Translational Science (NCATS). Its contents are solely the responsibility of the authors and do not necessarily represent the official views of the NIH. Author Contributions Kenny Y. Wang: investigation, data curation, and writing – original draft. Timothy T. Xu: conceptualization, investigation, methodology, project administration, writing – original draft, and review and editing. Launia J. White: formal analysis and visualization. Lauren A. Dalvin: writing – review and editing, supervision, and funding acquisition. Funding Statement This study was made possible using the resources of the Rochester Epidemiology Project, which is supported by the National Institute on Aging of the National Institutes of Health under Award Number R01AG034676. This publication was made possible through the support of the Leonard and Mary Lou Hoeft Career Development Award Fund in Ophthalmology Research, Grant No. P30 CA015083 from the National Cancer Institute, and CTSA Grant No. KL2 TR002379 from the National Center for Advancing Translational Science (NCATS). Its contents are solely the responsibility of the authors and do not necessarily represent the official views of the NIH. Data Availability Statement More detailed data than those presented in the manuscript are not publicly available due to ethical reasons. The data supporting the results are available upon reasonable request to the corresponding author. References 1. Tamboli D, Topham A, Singh N, Singh AD. Retinoblastoma: a SEER dataset evaluation for treatment patterns, survival, and second malignant neoplasms. Am J Ophthalmol. 2015;160(5):953–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Zabor EC, Radivoyevitch T, Singh AD, Kilic E, de Klein JEMM, Kalirai H, et al. Conditional survival in uveal melanoma. Ophthalmol Retina. 2021;5(6):536–42. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Pimentel MA, Browne EN, Janardhana PM, Borkar DS, Tham VM, Uchida A, et al. Assessment of the accuracy of using ICD-9 codes to identify uveitis, herpes zoster ophthalmicus, scleritis, and episcleritis. JAMA Ophthalmol. 2016;134(9):1001–6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Uchiyama E, Faez S, Nasir H, Unizony SH, Plenge R, Papaliodis GN, et al. Accuracy of the international classification of diseases, ninth revision, clinical modification (ICD-9-CM) as a research tool for identification of patients with uveitis and scleritis. Ophthalmic Epidemiol. 2015;22(2):139–41. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Muir KW, Gupta C, Gill P, Stein JD. Accuracy of international classification of diseases, ninth revision, clinical modification billing codes for common ophthalmic conditions. JAMA Ophthalmol. 2013;131(1):119–20. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ 