INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(361,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10174181','Social Media and Myeloproliferative Neoplasms (MPN)--Focus on Twitter and the Development of a Disease-specific Community: #MPNSM.','Pemmaraju N, Gupta V, Mesa R, Thompson MA.','2015','mpnsm pharmds instantaneously dameshek thrombocythemia dameshek hypereosinophilic w515l pikman curate asco15 ascoconnection subatomicdoc midyear uberflip mpnsm mpnsm naveen pemmaraju doctorpemm fischmd mtmdphd anas younes dranasyounes richardwjoseph muzaffar qazilbash transplant_doc doctopemm aacr aacr aacr14 tweeting goers energized doctorpemm leusm mpnsm healthhashtags symplur doctorpemm mpnsm hcsm btsm mmsm propelling mpnsm healthcarehashtags symplur galvanize ash_hematology ash14 hematologists mtmdphd vikas vikas_gupta_1 ruben mpdrc mpnsm mpnsm healthcarehashtags audun utengen audvin nha tmlfox luks hjluks symplur cto symplur chats symplur healthcarehashtags notices influencers healthhashtags symplur symplur symplur shorts utengen mpnsm symplur symplur mpnsm mpnsm fdate 2f01 2f2015 shour smin tdate 2f13 2f2015 thour tmin mpnsm mpnsm mpnsm mpnsm naveen pemmaraju doctorpemm mtmdphd vikas vikas ruben mpdrc moliterno mpncare fleischman mpnlab mpndoc gerds aarongerds micahelis lauracmichaelis navneet majhail bldcancerdoc muzaffar qazilbash transplantdoc perales drmiguelperales komanduri drkomanduri nitin clldoc tapan kadia tapkadia malin hultcrantz malinhultcrantz mohamad mohty mohty ebmt woodbd nazha aziznazhamd toyosi odenike myeloidmalig gaby hobs gabyhobbs mpnsm mpnsm mpnsm mpngroupcanada leukemiamda mpn_rf patientpower mayoleukemiamds symplur healthhashtags mpnsm tweeting mpnsm mpnsm veracity ash14 tweeted vanishing catalysts mpnsm csf3r cnl retweets favorites mpnsm setpb1 etnk1 tweeted retweeted eha aacr unfold debating ash14 asco15 eha20 aacr14 mpnsm pacritinib imetelstat pacritinib asco15 ruben mpdrc ashclinicalnews onclive retweeted tweeted favorites srdan verstovsek ruben imetelstat ash14 tefferi tweeted retweeted doctorpemm ash14 favorites imetelstat imetelstat favorites imetelstat favorites mpnsm retweeted tefferi mayoleukemiamds retweeted mpnsm partake mpnsm acquainted mpnsm mpdrc gaby gabyhobbs crti mpnsm mpnsm ca016672 creators cto symplur naveen pemmaraju lfb stemline vikas promedior ruben twitterverse baptist younes williston hawn scanfeld scanfeld attai cowher hamadani schoger staley landercasper e188 e14118 lazard scheinfeld suran glode gillman nwosu debattista vardiman arber dameshek speculations guilhot chronicphase kantarjian jabbour pemmaraju moliterno thrombocythaemia polycythaemia wadleigh cools thrombocythemia kralovics passamonti buser pikman mercher mplw515l e270 nangalia massie nonmutated klampfl gisslinger harutyunyan calreticulin cervantes dupriez verstovsek gotlib kiladjian niblack wadleigh mpds mpd geyer scherber dueck dueck geyer cartledge wasko vartabedian freiherr abdolrasulnia e117 basto perovic lawrentschuk utengen bahkali almaiman bahkali almaiman househ alsurimi debronkart demetri physicianpatient edbook_am melle kitching winbolt macphail jalali curating e101 edbook_am e101 duncombe scherber geyer scherber geyer malig maxson gotlib pollyea csf3r piazza valletta winkelmann setbp1 meggendorfer bacher alpermann setbp1 monosomy isochromosome gambacorti passerini donadoni parmiani etnk1 komrokji pacritinib sb1518 vannucchi kiladjian griesshammer tefferi lasho begna imetelstat baerlocher oppliger leibundgut ottmann imetelstat thrombocythemia armanios greider myeloproliferation dizon e114 edbook_am greysen','Abstract The advent of social media has led to the ability for individuals all over the world to communicate with each other, in real time, about mutual topics of interest in an unprecedented manner. Recently, the use of social media has increased among people interested in healthcare and medical research, particularly in the field of hematology and oncology, a field which frequently experiences rapid shifts of information and novel, practice-changing discoveries. Among the many social media platforms available to cancer patients and providers, one platform in particular, Twitter, has become the focus for the creation of disease-specific communities, especially for those interested in, affected by, or those who perform research in the fields of rare cancers, which historically have had a dearth of reliable information available. This article will focus on the initiation and progress of one such Twitter hematology/oncology community, #mpnsm, which was originally created for the purpose of serving as a venue for improving the interaction among patients, providers, researchers, and organizations with interest in the myeloproliferative neoplasms (MPNs) and to further the availability of reliable up-to-date analysis; relevant expert commentary; and readily usable information for patients, providers, and other groups interested in this field. Keywords: Social media, Twitter, Myeloproliferative neoplasm, Disease-specific hashtag, Myelofibrosis, Polycythemia vera Introduction The era of widespread adoption of social media as a crucial means of acquisition of new information, debate, and public discussion has opened an innovative conduit to increased communication among patients, providers, experts, advocates, and other interested parties involved in specified areas of medicine and health [ 1 , 2 ]. One of the medical areas that has experienced a profound growth of users is in the field of hematology and oncology [ 3 ]. Early adopters, pioneers, and so-called super-users including physicians in private practice, academic physicians, PharmDs, nurses, nurse practitioners, and physician assistants paved the way for other groups, such as patients, patient-based organizations/advocacy groups, community oncologists, and scientists/researchers to join the conversation and contribute to the social media medical landscape [ 4 ]. The use of social media, in particular Twitter, has led to increased ability for patients to communicate with each other about various medical topics [ 5 ], and, as demonstrated in some studies, Twitter usage has been associated with improved perceptions of patients’ knowledge of their specific disease areas [ 6 ]. Social media has allowed experts in the field to be able to more directly communicate their ideas with the rest of the world, focusing on, and reaching, those interested in mutually relevant areas of research among colleagues, outside of the traditional academic methods (medical literature, medical meetings, conferences, symposia) [ 1 ]. Furthermore, this medium has allowed for patients and patient groups to more directly communicate with doctors and thought leaders, ask questions, and instantaneously communicate, especially during times of disease outbreaks [ 7 , 8 ]. While there are many social media platforms available to users, the one that has gained some of the most positive attention among medical providers in the field of oncology and related fields is Twitter [ 9 , 10 ], which will be the focus of this article. Thompson et al. have outlined the basics of Twitter for the hematologist elsewhere in this journal’s issue and the reader is referred to this article for an introduction and overview of Twitter vocabulary and basics for the busy practitioner {reference: Thompson et al. article in this issue, to be added at the time of publication}. The myeloproliferative neoplasms (MPNs) represent a rare group of hematologic malignancies which involve malignant clonal hematopoiesis and heterogeneous phenotypic manifestations [ 11 ]. First characterized as a distinct family of diseases by William Dameshek in 1951, polycythemia vera (PV), essential thrombocythemia (ET), myelofibrosis (MF), and chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) were observed to share a similar set of clinical features [ 12 ]. Since Dameshek’s time, CML has subsequently been discovered to carry its own unique molecular signature and specified treatment strategies (Philadelphia chromosome, BCR-ABL positivity, Tyrosine kinase inhibitors) [ 13 , 14 ]. Besides these original, classic disease categories, a number of other rare MPNs have been subsequently included in this broad category of myeloid malignancies, such as primary hypereosinophilic disorders, systemic mastocytosis, and a separate category for MDS/MPNs, including atypical CML [ 11 ]. Over the past 10 years, there has been a striking increase in research breakthroughs and clinical developments for patients in the field of MPNs [ 15 ]. In 2005, several independent groups described a common molecular abnormality among the three classical MPNs (PV, ET, MF), known as the JAK2 V617F mutation, present in approximately 50–60 % of patients with ET and MF, and approximately 95–99 % of patients with PV [ 16 – 19 ]. Further research uncovered the MPL W515L somatic mutation by Pikman et al. in 2006 in patients with MF [ 20 ]. In 2013, two independent groups reported a newly described recurring molecular abnormality, CALR somatic mutation, which constitutes the second most common molecular abnormality occurring among patients with MPNs [ 21 , 22 ]. The elucidation of the role of JAK2 in MPNs, as well as other key biological discoveries, alongside the development of clinical scoring systems [ 23 ], have helped to lead to the increase in investigation for rational therapeutic targeting, drug development [ 24 , 25 ]. Furthermore, investigators in the field have gained insights into better understanding of risk stratification [ 15 ] and awareness of the importance of measuring and understanding the clinical/symptom burden for patients with MPNs [ 26 – 29 ]. With this rapid growth in terms of information in the MPNs over the past decade alone, and despite the wide availability of large amounts of information available online, there is still an urgent need for online communities to help organize, curate, and responsibly disseminate the most reliable, latest findings that impact patients in the field. Twitter has become an important venue for worldwide discussion of topical events, and recently, for medical education and information, discussion, and communication [ 30 ]. This medium allows interested users the ability for real-time interaction in a fully visible, easily accessible, archived/searchable format. Notably, Twitter has gained following among patients, doctors, and researchers interested in particularly rare diseases, including cancers, that traditionally may not have had much attention or resources dedicated as compared to more prevalent diseases. Many of these interested parties view social media as a novel, efficient tool for acquisition and dissemination of key pieces of newly developing medical information [ 31 ]. One significant development is the increased use of Twitter by academic physicians attending oncology conferences and the associated generation of information among conference attendees with the ability for interested people to join in real-time conversations, even among those who are not physically present at the meeting [ 32 ]. In an article for the #ASCO15 meeting earlier this year, writing for ASCOconnection.org , Dr. Matthew S. Katz, MD (@subatomicdoc) summarized the growing list of disease-specific hashtags recognized by the social media community as of midyear 2015 ( http://read.uberflip.com/i/503814-may-2015-asco-connection/28 ). The current article will focus on one particular area listed among these disease-specified areas, the MPNs, under the hashtag of #mpnsm, as an example of the use of Twitter for disease-focused communication, with the goal of bringing together all interested parties in a rare tumor field for ongoing discussion, collaboration, and dissemination of latest ideas and research. #MPNSM Inspiration and Founding Although there are many websites and social media venues dedicated to patients with MPNs, there was no single location featuring a dedicated space for communication among academic MPN-focused physicians and physician-researchers as of 2014. Naveen Pemmaraju, MD, an assistant professor of leukemia at MD Anderson Cancer Center, (@doctorpemm) first joined Twitter in March 2014, inspired by his colleagues Dr. Michael Fisch(@fischmd), Dr. Michael Thompson (@mtmdphd), Dr. Anas Younes (@DrAnasYounes), Dr. Richard Joseph (@RichardWJoseph), and Dr. Muzaffar Qazilbash (@Transplant_Doc). @doctopemm’s first tweets were from the AACR (@AACR) 2014 Annual Meeting (San Diego, #AACR14). Tweeting during the meeting increased his level of learning of the material presented, facilitated interactions with a wide range of collaborators/researchers (some of whom were not able to attend the meeting), and led to networking/interactions with other conference-goers who he would not have met otherwise. Energized by the amplified learning and networking from that experience, @doctorpemm began to be a regular Twitter user from that point forward, with the goal of focusing on disseminating the latest research developments in the fields of leukemia (#leusm) and MPN. Official Launch of #Mpnsm: @Healthhashtags, Symplur.com The authors have all found Twitter to be an outstanding method for obtaining medical information and also for contributing disease-focused information. In August 2014, @doctorpemm sent the first tweet under the hashtag #mpnsm (Myeloproliferative Neoplasms on Social Media). This event was inspired by the success of the Cancer Tag Ontology system [ 33 ] based on #hcsm and #btsm, launched in July 2013 [ 33 ]. In terms of malignant hematology-focused communities, the success of #mmsm (multiple myeloma) and participation by several of the authors in this community also aided in propelling the start of the #mpnsm community. Finally, our colleagues at the @healthcarehashtags project ( Symplur.com ) have helped to galvanize, organize, and analyze this new disease-specific focused community. By the time of the American Society of Hematology (ASH) (@ASH_hematology) 2014 Annual Meeting (#ASH14) in December of 2014, there was great momentum building among hematologists for the use of Twitter, and many new hematology users signed on to Twitter during this key period. With a goal to foster and facilitate dialogue and commentary in the field of MPNs across the world, and with the help and support of co-founders Dr. Michael Thompson (@mtmdphd), Dr. Vikas Gupta (@Vikas_Gupta_1), and Dr. Ruben Mesa (@mpdrc), the authors were able to formally launch #mpnsm, and we submitted #mpnsm has an official hashtag entry to @healthcarehashtags. This hashtag was accepted and posted to Twitter by January 31, 2015 as a new health hashtag/disease category. Now used regularly for day-to-day updates for major papers and therapeutic developments in the field, as well as at times of MPN-related conferences and major medical meetings, individuals interested in MPNs have a chance to be a part of an ongoing, archived conversation on Twitter. Initiated in October 2010, by co-founders Audun Utengen (@audvin), Thomas M. Lee, BS, NHA (@tmlfox), and Howard J. Luks (@hjluks), the Healthcare Hashtags Project as part of Symplur.com was created. In combination with the Cancer Tag Ontology (CTO) program developed in July 2013, these two systems have given rise to the creation of disease-specific hashtags and, therefore, communities focused on a vast array of focused medical topics available for all interested individuals and groups to participate in real-time discussions ( http://www.symplur.com/blog/healthcare-hashtags-social-project/ ) [ 33 ]. The forums provided by Twitter provide users with a plethora of different categories for disease areas, including Tweet Chats/Tweet-ups, conferences, and diseases areas. As of September 10, 2015, according to Symplur.com , the @healthcarehashtags project as a social media platform has featured over 3047 contributors; 7850 hashtags; 15,237 topics; and over 1,072,282,839 tweets. The site monitors the top trending topics and contributors and regularly posts notices on its Twitter feed for new health hashtags and for “Top Influencers” of each hashtag ( https://twitter.com/healthhashtags ). According to Symplur.com , an impression is a Twitter-specific metric that has served as a useful measure for quantifying the impact of tweets in a specific area. Specifically, the impression is a measure of the impact of a particular healthcare hashtag in a Twitter user’s tweet stream. The team at Symplur.com calculates the total impression index by using a formula which takes the number of tweets per participant and multiplying it with the number of followers that a participant has at that time; this calculation is performed for all participants during the specified time period and the summation of these calculations is reported as the total impression index. ( http://www.symplur.com/shorts/new-healthcare-analytics-features-social-media/ ; personal communication, A. Utengen September 2015). With regards to the #mpnsm community analytics, according to Symplur.com (accessed September 13, 2015); from the period of January 1, 2015 to September 13, 2015, there have been 2013 tweets from 285 participants, resulting in 4,049,415 impressions ( http://www.symplur.com/healthcare-hashtags/mpnsm/analytics/?hashtag=mpnsm&fdate=01%2F01%2F2015&shour=00&smin=00&tdate=09%2F13%2F2015&thour=00&tmin=00 ). Sampling of Users of #Mpnsm A wide range of users regularly follow, tweet, and are mentioned on #mpnsm. This growing list of users includes patients, patient advocates, medical professionals, and basic and translational scientists/researchers interested in MPNs. Table 1 demonstrates a selection of physician-investigators in the field of malignant hematology with dedicated focus to patients and/or research in the MPN field. Importantly, this list is not meant to be an exhaustive/comprehensive list; instead, its aim is to provide a starting point or a sampling of physician-researchers, who practice at academic centers throughout the world for the reader to have an idea of some of the users in the field of MPNs who either use or have been mentioned on #mpnsm. Table 1. Selection of physician-investigators in the field of MPNs using/or mentioned on #mpnsm Physician Twitter handle Naveen Pemmaraju, MD @doctorpemm Mike Thompson, MD,PhD @mtmdphd Vikas Gupta, MD @Vikas Gupta 1 Ruben Mesa, MD @mpdrc Alison Moliterno, MD @mpncare Angela Fleischman, MD, PhD @MPNlab Stephen Oh, MD, PhD @mpndoc Aaron Gerds, MD @AaronGerds Laura C. Micahelis, MD @lauracmichaelis Navneet Majhail, MD @BldCancerDoc Muzaffar Qazilbash, MD @TransplantDoc Miguel Perales, MD @DrMiguelPerales Krishna Komanduri, MD @drkomanduri Nitin Jain, MD @CLLdoc Tapan Kadia, MD @TapKadia Malin Hultcrantz, MD @malinhultcrantz Mohamad Mohty, MD @Mohty EBMT Bill Wood, MD @WoodBD Aziz Nazha, MD @AzizNazhaMD Toyosi Odenike, MD @myeloidmalig Gaby Hobs, MD @GabyHobbs Open in a new tab Table 2 shows a selection of organizations/groups that regularly use, mentioned on, or tweet under #mpnsm for communication of upcoming clinical trials, educational videos, or MPN-related events. Again, this is not intended to be a comprehensive list of this growing group, but instead meant to highlight some entities and organizations for the reader to have an idea of some of the groups who are active, or have been mentioned, on Twitter under the hashtag of #mpnsm. Table 2. Selected organizations and groups in the field of MPNs using/or mentioned on #mpnsm Organization/group Twitter handle Canadian MPN Group @MPNGroupCanada MD Anderson Leukemia @LeukemiaMDA MPN Research Foundation @MPN_RF Patient Power @PatientPower Mayo Leukemia-MDS @MayoLeukemiaMDS Symplur Hashtags @healthhashtags Open in a new tab Areas of Application/Future Directions The growth of disease-specific hashtags has led to a new era of possibilities for connecting all interested groups of rare diseases in an online community setting. In the first year of #mpnsm, an establishment of a sustained group of interested physician-scientists, patients, and organizations has been demonstrated; the dissemination of important journal articles in the field have been posted and featured real-time debate and discussion among thought leaders; and tweeting before, during, and after major hematology conferences has allowed for unprecedented real-time communication and networking of ideas. In the second year of #mpnsm and beyond, further emphasis will likely be placed on identifying and nurturing new hematology Twitter users dedicated to MPNs, establishing a regular chat discussion forum (called a “Tweet-up”) on a monthly basis for an in-depth review of specified sub-topics, and continuing outreach and dissemination of key educational materials (videos, links to peer-reviewed publications, expert opinions, and commentaries). Social media gives individuals the opportunity to reach and engage large audiences and has the potential to provide access to high-quality, expert-based information in a cost-effective manner, which is a very important aspect especially for resource-constrained areas of the world [ 34 ]. Dissemination of Latest Findings via Original Manuscripts and Review Articles During the busy hematologist’s week, the varying demands of private practice, teaching, and in academic research are leading to a significant reduction in time during the physician’s week for reading medical journals [ 1 ]. Despite the widespread availability of medical information via the Internet, most practitioners feel overwhelmed with the sheer amount of material to keep up within any given month [ 1 ]. One helpful aspect of Twitter has been to act as a personalized information filter, created by the end-user him/herself, in this case, a physician who can assign individuals and entities he or she wants to follow. In so doing, a user can create a continuous stream of information tailored for any disease sub-group or category. In the case of MPNs, if a user follows a disease-focused community, such as #mpnsm, one of the advantages of this is being exposed to information in a topic that the user may not have ever come across on his or her own. For example, reading an important post from a journal article that is not from one of the major journals available could lead to finding a key aspect important to the doctor’s practice or an academic researchers’ field much more quickly than traditionally expected. Also, viewing a tweet with analysis and commentary from a key opinion leader’s posting of a recent major journal article may allow the user more insight and opportunity for learning, than simply reading the article on his or her own. These aspects of social media may actually lead to increased understanding and knowledge for the physician, and lead to a greater use of already constrained time when attempting to keep up with the latest developments in one’s field of interest but must be balanced with a constant vigilance for veracity of the information available, preserved ability to read more detailed versions/original source texts, and maintenance of patient privacy and medical professionalism [ 35 ]. Another crucial group in terms of acquisition of medical information is among trainees. Twitter usage is increasingly becoming adopted by medical students, residents, and fellows during the course of their training and education [ 36 ], although the legitimacy and standardization of the information being obtained often requires a high level of vigilance in terms of accuracy [ 37 ]. As the field of social media intersecting with medical education continues to evolve, Twitter may provide trainees with opportunities to follow and, ultimately, contribute to specific areas of research and provide a venue for mentees to find mentors and for initiation of research collaborations. Future research will be important to determine the ability to disseminate reliable, relevant information, and the various metrics/levels of impact for both medical educators as well as on the trainees, especially in sub-speciality training programs with detailed and focused areas of rapidly changing knowledge, such as hematology/oncology training [ 38 , 39 ]. Clinical Trial Information and Awareness One potential important use of the Twitter community, especially for rare diseases such as the MPNs, is for gathering up-to-date, reliable information regarding clinical trials for patients and referring providers [ 40 ]. Because clinical trial enrollment remains very low among adults with cancer in the USA (some estimates as low as 5 % of adult cancer patient) [ 41 ], there is urgent need for education and information in this area. Clinical trial recruitment and Twitter updates can be created to reach various audiences, as long as this is in compliance with local/institutional and other regulatory/IRB entities, including but not limited to alerts to patients and families, community hematologist/oncologists, academic physician-researchers, and other institutions/organizations/groups, which can include, for example, a www.clinicaltrials.gov link for a particular clinical trial [ 3 ]. Finally, as was seen at #ASH14, continued increase in Twitter usage and disease-specified hashtags are being created for each of the major medical conferences and the sub-group tumor specific meetings held throughout the year, with details of clinical trials (both recently completed and ongoing trials) tweeted about during the course of the year, before, during, and after medical conferences. MPN Symptom Burden One potential use for Twitter and social media will be for the discussion of symptom burden in MPNs. Described by Mesa and colleagues, the symptoms associated with some of the MPNs can lead to decreased quality of life, significant loss of work-years/productivity, and unrecognized burden to the patient and the healthcare system [ 42 , 43 ]. Twitter is one of the online social media resources readily available to many patients as a way for patients and patient groups to connect with each other in real time, to discuss these symptoms, and to reach out to healthcare providers and researchers to ask questions and communicate important aspects of the diseases themselves that may not always be captured in the standard, ever-vanishing, time-constrained clinic visit. As the need for more accurate methodologies to record, measure, and address symptom burden arises among patients with MPN, it will be vital to provide patients with a myriad of tools and vehicles with which to document their symptoms during the course of the disease and with various therapies [ 44 ] and social media may serve as one of the resources/catalysts in the near future for patients’ reporting and communication in this area of MPN healthcare. Developments in Disease Biology/New Molecular Findings One important use for #mpnsm and the establishment of a Twitter community is the rapid dissemination of new molecular findings in the MPNs. Traditionally, these new findings would be first presented in disease-specific academic meetings then larger broad-based conferences and then subsequently published in the medical literature, typically not readily accessible to most of the general public for some time. For example, the discovery and elucidation of CSF3R mutations in patients with chronic neutrophilic leukemia (CNL) and atypical CML [ 45 ] was immediately posted by @NEJM and received 25 retweets and 13 favorites, constituting a large number of people viewing this information in an otherwise rare tumor type. With the initiation of and widespread usage of the #mpnsm hashtag approximately 1 year later, providers and patient/patient groups have been able to continue the discussion with regard to this rare tumor, and subsequent newer molecular discoveries in this area (for example, first descriptions of recurring abnormalities: SETPB1 [ 46 , 47 ] and ETNK1 mutations [ 48 ]) have now been tweeted, retweeted (RT), and discussed in real time, again reaching a much wider readership than previously possible. Real-time Discussion of Novel Therapeutic Agents and Clinical Trial Results One of the uses of Twitter with the most impact in medical communities has been the real-time discussion among those in the field when new clinical trial results are announced at larger meetings, such as ASH, ASCO, EHA, and AACR. During these presentations and afterwards, individuals can tweet questions and contribute his/her own thoughts, and a conversation can then unfold debating various aspects of the newly released data, now organized by conference-specific hashtags (for example, #ASH14, #ASCO15, #EHA20, #AACR14). With regard to #mpnsm, two recent examples of this phenomenon include discussions of results for pacritinib, an oral JAK2/FLT3 dual inhibitor [ 49 ] and imetelstat, a novel telomerase inhibitor. In the case of pacritinib, the PERSIST-1 trial results were announced at #ASCO15; these results were presented by Dr. Ruben Mesa (@mpdrc) during an oral presentation on May 30, 2015 at the annual conference. Many investigators and several organizations (for example, @ASHClinicalNews, @OncLive) were able to tweet and be retweeted on that day, with discussions about specific elements of the study, including side effect profile, spleen size reductions featured in the tweets, and individuals had the opportunity to ask Dr Mesa further questions on Twitter that they may not have the opportunity to do otherwise during the meeting. Another example is that of clinical trial results for ruxolitinib for patients with advanced PV. Tweeted by @NEJM on January 28, 2015 (46 RT, 26 favorites), the results of the RESPONSE trial [ 50 ] were able to be discussed in real time, with in-depth interviews posted to Twitter with investigators in the field (Dr. Srdan Verstovsek, Dr. Ruben Mesa, Dr. Claire Harrison, etc.) in the subsequent days for analysis of side effects, outcome data, symptom burden, and clinical implications. An even more recent example is that of imetelstat. The preliminary results were presented at #ASH14 by Dr. A. Tefferi. These results were tweeted and retweeted during the meeting (as an example, one tweet on this topic from @doctorpemm, #ASH14, December 8,2014, received 5 RT and 3 favorites). Approximately 9 months later, @NEJM published, in one issue, two separate peer-reviewed manuscripts featuring imetelstat (ET, MF), along with an insightful editorial, and all three of these articles were posted to Twitter on September 5, 2015 [ 51 – 53 ] (@NEJM, Imetelstat for Myelofibrosis, 40 RT, 40 Favorites; @NEJM Imetelstat for ET, 31 RT, 28 favorites) and then subsequently taken up by the #mpnsm community and retweeted and commented upon by many groups, organizations, researchers, and patients. Key interviews, including one featuring Dr. Tefferi from @MayoLeukemiaMDS, was presented via Twitter and was retweeted via the #mpnsm hashtag for further visibility to a broader audience. Events like these, which are fully visible to all on Twitter, that feature new clinical trial drugs presented at either major national/international meetings or peer-reviewed publications, allow all interested parties to partake in debate and discussion in a way that was not possible in prior eras and can serve to maximize an individual’s understanding of the available information. Announcement of New Investigators to the Field A very helpful aspect of the Twitter-based disease-focused hashtag community such as #mpnsm is the immediate ability to be introduced to new investigators in the field. Generally, physicians and scientists who are not yet acquainted may not meet for long periods of time until they are introduced at a meeting or have read one another’s papers and connect in this manner. Twitter has allowed for the rapid connection of academic researchers with shared interests, especially for those studying patients with rare tumors. One recent example of this is the announcement to the #mpnsm community by @mpdrc of Dr. Gaby Hobbs (@GabyHobbs) on August 6, 2015, as Dr. Hobbs had just completed the ASH CRTI program. Tweets like this have the power to immediately inform the rest of the group of a new member to the MPN research community and simultaneously introduce the researcher to the #mpnsm format for their subsequent use and adoption. Discussion The continued, sustained use of social media in the fields of hematology and oncology is becoming recognized as a key aspect of the cancer patients and cancer provider’s care experience [ 54 ]. Twitter is one particular form of social media that has become a favored venue for both patients and providers involved in cancer care, particularly for the enhancement of learning, dissemination of knowledge, and exposure to expert opinions at the time of major medical meetings and conferences [ 32 ]. Because there are so many fields and now subfields within hematology/oncology, the introduction of the disease-specific and conference-specific hashtag has helped to organize interested parties around a definitive community located on Twitter and helped to increase the impact of critical information among all interested parties for maximum learning among users [ 33 , 55 ], using social media to learn and communicate. This has been particularly relevant and helpful for those patients, researchers, and providers who are interested in or affected by a chronic disease [ 56 ] or rare tumors that historically have not had a large amount of information readily available. By bringing together all interested groups who have mutual interest in these areas, Twitter oncology communities, such as #mpnsm, have become an important way for people affected by/or involved in the care or research of rare tumors, to stay connected, keep up to date on the latest developments, and provide a forum for networking and collaboration. Future studies are warranted to analyze in detail the direct impact of Twitter for the hematology/oncology patient’s outcomes, quality of life, and to understand the impact of social media on research and innovations in the field. Acknowledgments This research is supported in part by the MD Anderson Cancer Center Support Grant P30 CA016672. The authors thank Dr. Matthew Katz and the creators of the Cancer Ontology Tag (CTO) program for their example and inspiration, the founders and members of Symplur.com , and the Healthcare Hashtags Project for their continued analysis and support. Footnotes Conflict of Interest Naveen Pemmaraju reports honorarium/consulting and/or research and clinical trial support: Novartis, LFB, Incyte, Stemline. Vikas Gupta received research funding through institutions from Novartis, Incyte, Gilead, and Promedior; served on the advisory board for Novartis; and received honorarium/consulting fees from Novartis/Incyte. Ruben Mesa is the Editor-in-Chief of Current Hematologic Malignancy Reports . Michael A. Thompson declares no potential conflicts of interest. Human and Animal Rights and Informed Consent This article does not contain any studies with human or animal subjects performed by any of the authors. References 1. Savage N Scientists in the Twitterverse. Cell. 2015;162:233–4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Baptist AP, Thompson M, Grossman KS, Mohammed L, Sy A, Sanders GM. Social media, text messaging, and email-preferences of asthma patients between 12 and 40 years old. J Asthma. 2011;48:824–30. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Thompson MA, Younes A, Miller RS. Using social media in oncology for education and patient engagement. Oncology (Williston Park). 2012;26:782, 4-5-91. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Hawn C Take two aspirin and tweet me in the morning: how Twitter, Facebook, and other social media are reshaping health care. Health Aff. 2009;28:361–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Scanfeld D, Scanfeld V, Larson EL. Dissemination of health information through social networks: twitter and antibiotics. Am J Infect Control. 2010;38:182–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Schol