arcinoma or adenocarcinoma histological subtype. In univariable analysis in the squamous cell carcinoma cohort, RT duration longer than 45 days (vs ≤45 days) was associated with inferior LRF and DFS (eTable 5 in Supplement 4 ). In univariable analysis in the adenocarcinoma cohort, RT duration longer than 45 days (vs ≤45 days) was not associated with outcomes (eTable 6 in Supplement 4 ). Outcomes for RT Duration as a Continuous Variable After controlling for an NRG Oncology trial, a 1-week increase in RT duration was associated with a 17% increased risk of having a DFS failure (HR, 1.17; 95% CI, 1.04-1.32; P = .009) and an LRF (HR, 1.17; 95% CI, 1.04-1.31; P = .01) (eTable 7 in Supplement 4 ). After controlling for an NRG Oncology trial, sex, Zubrod score, tumor size, N category, histological subtype, and 80% or greater protocol-specified concurrent chemotherapy, a 1-week increase in RT duration was associated with a 14% increased risk of having a DFS failure (HR, 1.14; 95% CI, 1.01-1.28; P = .03) ( Table 3 ). The HR for LRF was 1.13, but the results were not statistically significant (95% CI, 0.99-1.28; P = .07) ( Table 3 ). Table 3. Multivariable Models: Radiation Therapy Duration, Continuous (N = 509 Patients). Variable a OS (n = 409 deaths) DFS (n = 445 failures) LRF (n = 288 failures) DF (n = 219 failures) HR (95% CI) P value b HR (95% CI) P value b HR (95% CI) P value b HR (95% CI) P value b RT duration (continuous; unit increase = 7 d) 1.08 (0.97-1.22) .17 1.14 (1.01-1.28) .03 1.13 (0.99-1.28) .07 0.87 (0.72-1.03) .11 NRG Oncology trial RTOG 0436 1 [Reference] NA 1 [Reference] NA 1 [Reference] NA 1 [Reference] NA RTOG 8501 1.11 (0.85-1.46) .45 1.06 (0.80-1.40) .71 1.21 (0.91-1.62) .19 0.76 (0.52-1.11) .15 RTOG 9405 0.96 (0.73-1.26) .76 1.08 (0.82-1.42) .59 1.33 (0.99-1.78) .05 0.99 (0.68-1.44) .95 Age (continuous; unit increase = 10 y) NA NA 0.86 (0.77-0.97) .01 0.84 (0.73-0.97) .02 Sex Female 1 [Reference] NA 1 [Reference] NA NA NA NA NA Male 1.50 (1.14-1.96)