INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(408,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8514083','Novel JAK2 rearrangement resulting from a t(9;22)(p24;q11.2) in B-acute lymphoblastic leukemia.','Tirado CA, Chen W, Huang LJ, Laborde C, Hiemenz MC, Valdez F, Ho K, Winick N, Lou Z, Koduru P.','2010','p24 aall0232 portend p24 9p24 pseudokinase jh2 jh1 jh2 pseudokinase pseudokinase r683 p24 lymphoblasts cytometryic lymphoblasts cd10 cd22 cd79a tdt myeloperoxidase p24 172kb rp11 927h16 enzo 22q 22q11 igl 22q11 digeorge n25 22q11 arsa 22q13 ewsr1 22q12 arsa ewsr1 translocated 22q11 ewsr 22q12 tetra jh2 r683 bac rp11 927h16 aall0232 clorafabine cyclosphosphamide pseudokinase r683 p24 multipotent p22 p24 pcm1 p24 p24 q21 p24 rpn1 nfe2 q25 p24 p24 unfavorably karyotypic ssbp2 pcm1 22q11 ewrs1 22q12 chek2 22q12 p24 p24 p24 pax5 p24 ssbp2 p22 p24 pcm1 9p24 r683 r683 ikzf1 ikaros portend saga wadleigh cools thrombocythemia ugo couedic polycythaemia mullighan lacronique boureux cools walz watmore p22 p24 fuses pcm1 poitras aster ssbp2 q14 p24 nebral denk attarbaschi pax5 knudson gangat 9p24 jak2v617f','Abstract Rearrangements of JAK2 are rare and have been described in various hematological neoplasms. We report a novel JAK2 rearrangement resulting from a t(9;22)(p24;q11.2) in a 14-year-old male with a diagnosis of B lymphoblastic leukemia. He was treated with Children’s Oncology Group’s protocol (AALL0232) but failed to achieve remission by day 29. He underwent a second induction and entered remission. His clinical course suggested that this JAK2 rearrangement might portend an unfavorable prognosis. This case brings the total number of JAK2 rearranged lymphoblastic leukemia cases in the literature to seven. The molecular genetic and clinicopathologic features of these cases were reviewed. Keywords: JAK2 rearrangement, t(9;22)(p24;q11.2), B-lymphoblastic leukemia 1. Introduction JAK2 , belonging to the Janus family of kinases, is located on chromosome 9p24 and encodes a cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase involved in cytokine-mediated signaling [ 1 ]. JAK2 contains an N-terminal segment followed by a pseudokinase domain (JH2) and a C-terminal tyrosine kinase domain (JH1). The JH2 pseudokinase domain is predicted to exert an inhibitory role on the kinase domain. Gain of function mutations and translocations involving JAK2 and subsequent activation of the JAK2 kinase have been described in various hematological neoplasms [ 1 ]. The mutations in the JAK2 pseudokinase domain, V617F and R683, are predicted to disrupt negative autoregulation and are present in the majority of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms [ 2 , 3 ] and a small subset of high-risk acute lymphoblastic leukemia [ 4 ], respectively. However, JAK2 rearrangements are rare, with only about 20 cases of myeloid and lymphoid neoplasms described in the literature [ 5 – 10 ]. Herein, we present a case of 14-year-old male with a diagnosis of B-lymphoblastic leukemia and with a novel JAK2 rearrangement resulting from a t(9;22)(p24;q11.2). Our case brings the total number of JAK2 rearranged lymphoblastic leukemia cases in the literature to 7. The molecular genetic and clinicopathologic features of these cases were reviewed. 2. Case report A 14-year-old Hispanic male presented with increasing weakness and decreased appetite. Laboratory examination revealed hyperleukocytosis (white blood cell count of 253,000 × 10 9 /L), anemia (hemoglobin 3.3 g/L) and thrombocytopenia (platelet count of 36,000 × 10 9 /L). There was a predominant population of lymphoblasts in both peripheral blood and bone marrow specimens. Immunophenotypic analysis by 4-colour flow cytometryic analysis on peripheral blood revealed an 80% of B-lymphoblasts expressing CD34, CD10, CD19, CD20(partial), CD22, CD33(partial), CD79a, HLA-DR and Tdt but lacking myeloperoxidase, which was consistent with a B-lymphoblastic leukemia/lymphoma. Chromosomal analysis on the peripheral blood sample revealed 46,XY,t(9;22)(p24;q11.2)[14]/46,XY[ 6 ]. The JAK2 rearrangement was confirmed by fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) analysis using a 172Kb bacterial artificial chromosome probe (RP11–927H16, Enzo Life Sciences International, Inc) that covers the entire JAK2 , which demonstrated separation of one of the JAK2 genes on derivative chromosomes 9 and 22 ( Fig. 1 ). In addition, the FISH sub-telomere set for chromosome 9 showed translocation of the one 9p signal to the der(22q). In an effort to map the molecular break at 22q11.2, a panel of FISH analyses were performed. The BCR and the IGL probes at 22q11.2 showed normal signal pattern and remained at the germline position. The DiGeorge probe set [ N25 (22q11.2) and ARSA (22q13)] and the EWSR1 probe (22q12) showed normal signal patterns, but the entire signals of ARSA and EWSR1 were translocated to the der(9p). These results suggest that the molecular break of the partner gene occurred distal to the BCR (22q11.2) and proximal to the EWSR (22q12). Further molecular studies on JAK2 by tetra-primer amplification refractory mutation system (ARMS)-PCR [ 2 ] and direct sequencing for exons 2–16 (encompassing the N-terminal to JH2 domain) showed no evidence of V617F or R683 mutations or any abnormalities within theses exons. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab FISH analysis using the BAC RP11–927H16 probe covering the entire JAK2 locus shows a signal on the normal copy of chromosome 9, a second signal on the derivative chromosome 9 and a third signal on the derivative 22, indicating JAK2 rearrangement. The patient was started on the induction chemotherapy using the Children’s Oncology Group’s protocol (AALL0232) but failed to achieve a remission by day 29. He underwent a second induction with clorafabine, etoposide and cyclosphosphamide, entered remission, and subsequently received an allogeneic stem cell transplant from an HLA-matched sibling. He remains in remission at 6 months follow-up. 3. Discussion Constitutive activation of JAK2 by point mutations in the pseudokinase domain, V617F and R683, is present frequently in chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms [ 2 , 3 ] and a small subset of high-risk lymphoblastic leukemias [ 4 ], respectively. Alternatively, JAK2 activation is achieved through gene fusions encoding chimeric proteins in which the kinase domain of JAK2 is fused to other cellular genes that provide dimerization interface to the JAK2 kinase domain, leading to growth-independent activation [ 5 ]. Compared to JAK2 point mutations, JAK2 rearrangement is rare with only about 20 reported cases. The spectrum of disease, however, is much more diverse, likely related to variable partner genes [ 5 – 10 ]. Moreover, even single fusion genes, such as t(9;12)(p24;p13)/ ETV6-JAK2 , are associated with varied disease presentations, implying that JAK2 rearranged neoplasm likely arises from multipotent cells with stem cell properties. Currently approximately 10 partner genes/translocations have been identified, including t(8;9)(p22;p24)/ PCM1-JAK2 , t(9;22)(p24;q11)/ BCR-JAK2 , t(9;12)(p24;p13)/ ETV6-JAK2 , t(3;9)(q21;p24)/ RPN1-JAK2 , der(9)t(9;12)/ NFE2-JAK2 , t(4;9)(q25;p24) and t(2;9)(p21;p24) in atypical chronic myeloid leukemia, myelofibrosis, myelodysplastic syndrome/myeloproliferative neoplasms, acute myeloid leukemia and lymphoblastic leukemia [ 6 , 7 , 10 ]. Four gene fusions been described to date in 6 patients with lymphoblastic leukemia ( Table 1 ). Our case brings the total number cases with a novel JAK2 rearrangement to seven. Importantly, a combined analysis on these cases reveals distinct clinicopathologic and molecular genetic features ( Table 1 ). JAK2 rearranged leukemia is present in either B- or T-lymphoblastic leukemia with predominance of B-lymphoblastic leukemia. The age of patients is varied from 1 to 50 years with a striking male predominance. Patients often presented with hyperleukocytosis and responded unfavorably to chemotherapy with frequent relapse, and about one third of patients died from the disease during 33 months of follow-up. Our patient failed to achieve complete remission on day 29 but responded to a second induction and then underwent allogeneic transplant. Among four patients with karyotypic results, all patients had either a sole JAK2 translocation or simple cytogenetic abnormalities, suggesting that JAK2 rearrangement may play an important role in the pathogenesis of lymphoblastic leukemia. As predicted by the mechanism by which JAK2 is activated through translocation, many of these partner genes, ETV6 , SSBP2 and PCM1 , encode a polypeptide that is capable of providing dimerization/oligomerization interface to the JAK2 kinase domain. While we are not able to identify the partner gene located distal to BCR on 22q11.2 and proximal to EWRS1 on 22q12, due to sample limitation, a gene of interest possessing this functional domain is CHEK2 (22q12.1). This gene has been implicated in the pathogenesis of breast cancer. Table 1. Clinicopathologic and molecular genetic features of 7 patients with B- or T-lymphoblastic leukemia harboring JAK2 rearrangement. Number of patients (reference) Age (year)/sex WBC × 10 9 /L Karyotype Fusion gene CR/relapse Survival D/A (month) 1 [present case] 14/M 253 t(9;22)(p24;q11.2) ?-JAK2 Yes/no A (7+, SCT) 1 [ 6 ] 1.6/M 550 t(9;12)(p24;p13) ETV6-JAK2 Yes/yes A (33+) 1 [ 5 ] a 4/M NA del(6), t(9;12)(p24;p13) ETV6-JAK2 Yes/yes NA 2 [ 9 ] children/NA NA NA PAX5-JAK2 Yes/yes b A (30+) b 1 [ 8 ] 39/M 400 t(5;9)(p14.1;p24.1) SSBP2-JAK2 Yes/yes D (8) 1 [ 7 ] 50/M NA t(8;9)(p22;p24) PCM1-JAK2 No D (1) Open in a new tab CR, complete remission; D/A, dead/alive; M, male; NA, not available; SCT, allogeneic stem cell transplant. a All had a diagnosis of B-lymphoblastic leukemia except for this case with a T-lymphoblastic leukemia. b Information on other patient not available. Because of the JAK2 /9p24 chromosomal abnormality, we also examined for JAK2 V617F or R683 mutations or any abnormalities within the exons 2–16. No abnormality was detected, suggesting again that the JAK2 fusion transcript is important in the pathogenesis of lymphoblastic leukemia. Interestingly, the JAK2 R683 mutation, found in a subset of high-risk lymphoblastic leukemias is frequently associated with lymphoid transcription factor IKZF1 (IKAROS) alteration [ 4 ]. Given the lack of effective therapies and the aggressive clinical course of lymphoblastic leukemia harboring JAK2 abnormalities, it may be worthwhile to consider inclusion of patients with refractory leukemia in clinical trials using JAK2- selective inhibitors. In summary, our case combined with those reported indicates that JAK2 rearrangement is rare but a recurrent cytogenetic abnormality that may portend unfavorable clinical outcomes. Further studies of similar cases are needed to elucidate the mechanism of pathogenesis and the impact on survival, and to identify novel therapeutic targets. Footnotes Conflict of interest All authors declare no conflict of interest. References [1]. Smith CA, Fan G. The saga of JAK2 mutations and translocations in hematologic disorders: pathogenesis, diagnostic and therapeutic prospects, and revised World Health Organization diagnostic criteria for myeloproliferative neoplasms. Hum Pathol 2008;39:795–810. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [2]. Levine RL, Wadleigh M, Cools J, et al. Activating mutation in the tyrosine kinase JAK2 in polycythemia vera, essential thrombocythemia, and myeloid metaplasia with myelofibrosis. Cancer Cell 2005;7:387–97. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [3]. James C, Ugo V, Le Couedic JP, et al. A unique clonal JAK2 mutation leading to constitutive signalling causes polycythaemia vera. Nature 2005;434:1144–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [4]. Mullighan CG, Zhang J, Harvey RC, et al. JAK mutations in high-risk childhood acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A 2009;106:9414–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [5]. Lacronique V, Boureux A, Valle VD, et al. A TEL-JAK2 fusion protein with constitutive kinase activity in human leukemia. Science 1997;278:1309–12. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [6]. Peeters P, Raynaud SD, Cools J, et al. Fusion of TEL, the ETS-variant gene 6 (ETV6), to the receptor-associated kinase JAK2 as a result of t(9;12) in a lymphoid and t(9;15;12) in a myeloid leukemia. Blood 1997;90:2535–40. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [7]. Reiter A, Walz C, Watmore A, et al. The t(8;9)(p22;p24) is a recurrent abnormality in chronic and acute leukemia that fuses PCM1 to JAK2. Cancer Res 2005;65:2662–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [8]. Poitras JL, Dal Cin P, Aster JC, et al. Novel SSBP2-JAK2 fusion gene resulting from a t(5;9)(q14.1;p24.1) in pre-B acute lymphocytic leukemia. Genes Chromosomes Cancer 2008;47:884–9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [9]. Nebral K, Denk D, Attarbaschi A, et al. Incidence and diversity of PAX5 fusion genes in childhood acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Leukemia 2009;23:134–43. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [10]. Patnaik MM, Knudson RA, Gangat N, et al. Chromosome 9p24 abnormalities: prevalence, description of novel JAK2 translocations, JAK2V617F mutation analysis and clinicopathologic correlates. Eur J Haematol 2010;March:1–7 [Epub ahead of print]. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',28,'2025-10-03 13:56:21','2025-10-03 13:56:21');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(409,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6699877','Embolization of Chest Neoplasms: The Next Frontier in Interventional Oncology?','Lorenz JM, Navuluri R.','2019','tare tare tinged shrink gelfoam glue cautioned shunting shunting shunting feeders hypertrophied intercostobronchial shunting acryl mainstem nonbronchial feeders feeders intercostal phrenic feeder noninfused cavitary abutting embolized intercostobronchial intercostal embolized kucukay rebleeding shunting feeders cavitary embolized acryl feeders embolize feeders marcelin bronchoscopic marcelin pseudoaneurysm hepasphere sponge bronchoscopic endobronchial endobronchial salamonsen endobronchial endobronchial endobronchial bronchogenic mainstem endobronchial arteriogram hypervascularity embolized acryl metastasizing hilum mucoepidermoid angiosarcoma defunctionalize aorticopulmonary shrink racemose hilum diligent intraprocedural intraprocedural embolized nonbronchial feeders embolized nonbronchial feeders tpce transpulmonary tpce vogl primaries tpce tpce tare kennoki epirubicin sorafenib tare ricke y90 tare y90 shunting intraprocedural uncontrollable tightness tare tare gregorio medrano mainar rengel bronconeumol s1579 jesudasan thangakunam keshava bronchoscopic bronchology pulmonol lbr 0b013e3181da2faf moritani matsunaga nonsmall shimono s11604 sugiura tatsumi racemose haemangioma meandering thoraxjnl kucukay melih topcuoglu alpar murat altay acryl embosphere s00270 sueyoshi s00270 kennoki yuki maj kumpe aortopulmonary jilani s002700010062 marcelin soussan desmots razazi parrot haemoptysis nonsmall centeno haemoptysis pseudoaneurysm guglielmi detachable crj phrenic jcrt jcrt_742_17 salamonsen tazi mezalek lisbona endobronchial fermanis hlc maehara tsushima metastasizing s11604 yamane toyooka mucoepidermoid s00595 perrotta cerqua cammarata kakegawa kawashima angiosarcoma atcs bucourt streitparth teichgraber polyvinyl e29 e29 zangan leef nonsmall jcrt jcrt_93_17 e12866 ricke amthauer y90 s00270','Abstract The management of chest tumors and their sequelae has been an uncommon indication for transarterial embolization (TAE). More recently, vascular embolization has been increasingly performed for this indication. The most common reported indication for embolization of neoplastic disease in the chest is the control of bleeding resulting either from iatrogenic causes or from tumor invasion into a bronchus or vessel. A natural extension of the application of TAE to neoplasm-related hemoptysis is its burgeoning indication as a possible primary treatment for benign chest tumors, primary lung neoplasms, and metastatic disease in patients that are refractory to systemic therapies and have limited or no surgical options. The goals for this indication are tumor regression and management of bulk-related symptoms. In addition to bland TAE for this indication, authors have reported very initial results applying transarterial chemoembolization (TACE) and transarterial radioembolization (TARE) to chest neoplasms with promising results that support feasibility and safety. This article is an up-to-date review of the management of chest tumors with embolization and its variants. Keywords: Interventional radiology, lung cancer, embolization, chemoembolization, radioembolization, hemoptysis Historically, the management of chest tumors and their sequelae has been an uncommon indication for transarterial embolization (TAE), accounting for 6.6% of cases in a series of 401 cases. 1 More recently, vascular embolization has been increasingly performed for this indication. The most common reported indication for embolization of neoplastic disease is the control of bleeding resulting either from iatrogenic causes 2 or from tumor invasion into a bronchus or vessel. Support for this indication resides both in older, retrospective series that review TAE for benign and malignant indications and more recently, retrospective series focusing on hemoptysis caused by chest malignancy. A natural extension of the application of TAE to neoplasm-related hemoptysis is its burgeoning indication as a possible primary treatment for benign chest tumors, primary lung neoplasms, and metastatic disease in patients that are refractory to systemic therapies and have limited or no surgical options. The goals for this indication are tumor regression and management of bulk-related symptoms. In addition to bland TAE for this indication, authors have reported very initial results applying transarterial chemoembolization (TACE) and transarterial radioembolization (TARE) to chest neoplasms with promising results that support feasibility and safety. This article is an up-to-date review of the management of chest tumors with embolization and its variants. TAE for Management of Hemoptysis The most common reported indication of TAE for neoplastic disease is control of hemoptysis caused by primary lung neoplasms and pulmonary metastatic disease. In most cases, patients require palliation for inoperable malignancy. While TAE for interstitial lung disease is commonly reserved for massive hemoptysis, many authors routinely use TAE for all levels of tumor-related hemoptysis, including mild (blood-tinged sputum), moderate (episodes of more significant bleeding < 300 cc/d) and massive (>300 cc/d). 3 4 5 The technique for embolization in the setting of tumor-related hemoptysis is similar to the technique for non-neoplastic disease. Since the goal is to reduce the arterial pressure-head to control bleeding rather than to shrink tumor, clinical success has been reported with a wide variety of embolic agents, including coils, 6 gelfoam, 4 particles, 7 8 9 10 glue 11 or a combination. 3 12 Some authors have cautioned that shunting is present in over 30% of patients with malignant disease. 13 Shunting from systemic to pulmonary arteries is quite common and does not preclude embolization with particles in most cases ( Fig. 1 ). Shunting from systemic arteries to pulmonary veins can result in paradoxical embolization. Careful preliminary CTA and selected angiography should be performed to identify all potential feeders and to prevent complications ( Fig. 2 ). Finally, unlike embolization for benign disease, obstruction and bleeding from tumor invading the main pulmonary arteries or the central veins may require concurrent placement of an uncovered or covered stent, depending on the indication. 14 Fig. 1. Open in a new tab ( a ) A large adenocarcinoma is fed by a hypertrophied bronchial artery (arrow) that arises from an intercostobronchial trunk in a patient with hemoptysis. ( b ) Early venous-phase image shows shunting from the bronchial artery to a pulmonary artery (arrow). Embolization of the bronchial artery was performed using tris acryl spheres without complications. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Demonstration of variant bronchial artery anatomy during a planning CT angiogram prior to embolization. A right-sided bronchial artery (arrowhead) originates from the normal location at the level of the left mainstem bronchus. Three anomalous bronchial arteries (arrows) originate from the aortic arch. CT angiography can identify bronchial and nonbronchial feeders prior to embolization. Bland TAE is the most commonly reported technique for embolization of tumor-related hemoptysis. The first goal is immediate technical and clinical success evidenced by cessation or significant reduction of hemoptysis. Technical success rates of greater than 90% are commonly reported, defined by successful embolization of targeted vessels. However, immediate clinical success rates are lower, ranging from 77 to 87%. 4 12 15 16 In two retrospective reviews of patients undergoing TAE for NSCLC complicated by hemoptysis, 4 15 immediate clinical success was achieved in 81.5% (22/27) and 87% (108/125) despite technical success rates exceeding 95%. One explanation for the drop-off from technical to clinical success is that multiple feeding vessels are recruited by neoplastic disease that could be missed during angiography. In our experience, compared with embolization for chronic interstitial disease, embolization for neoplastic disease more typically includes non-bronchial in addition to bronchial arteries ( Fig. 3 ), and neoplastic erosion into pulmonary arterial branches has been described 14 17 ( Fig. 4 ). In addition to common feeders such as bronchial, intercostal, internal mammary, and chest wall arteries, the inferior phrenic artery was a feeder of pulmonary metastases in over 9% of cases. 18 Anomalous bronchial arteries may be missed, especially the most common variety that originate from the aortic arch ( Figs. 2 and 4 ). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab ( a ) Noninfused CT shows a large cavitary squamous cell carcinoma (arrow) in the left lung, abutting the chest wall in a patient with hemoptysis. ( b ) The mass is supplied by branches of the lateral thoracic artery (arrow). ( c ) The feeding branches were embolized with coils. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab ( a ) Contrast-enhanced coronal CT image shows a mass representing squamous cell carcinoma occupying the right apex in a patient with hemoptysis. Pulmonary arterial branches (arrow) course through the mass. ( b ) An intercostobronchial trunk supplies a bronchial artery to pulmonary artery shunt (arrow). ( c ) An intercostal artery to pulmonary artery shunt is also present (arrow). ( d ) Despite embolization of both feeding arteries (arrow) hemoptysis persisted. ( e ) Pulmonary angiography shows two segmental branches in the region of the tumor. ( f ) Both pulmonary branches were embolized with coils (arrows) resulting in cessation of hemoptysis. After immediate clinical success, the next goal is durability, evidenced by assessment of recurrence rates and hemoptysis-free survival. In some retrospective studies that reviewed the use of TAE for cases of both non-neoplastic and neoplastic disease, authors noted an increased rate of recurrence for the latter. One would expect this outcome since pulmonary malignancy progresses more rapidly than non-neoplastic causes of hemoptysis such as chronic interstitial lung disease. Kucukay et al reviewed 174 patients after embolization using large particles (700–900 μm) for the indication of massive hemoptysis. 7 Primary lung cancer was the cause in 29.9% of cases, and the remainder had bronchiectasis, tuberculosis, or were idiopathic. The recurrence rate over the follow-up period was significantly higher for cancer-related hemoptysis (16.7% versus 4.0%: p = 0.01). Still, for purposes of palliation of malignant tumors with a high mortality that cannot be managed by alternative means, this recurrence rate is an acceptable outcome and is consistent with reported recurrence rates ranging from 10 to 25% in retrospective reviews that concentrate on malignant hemoptysis. In an observational, retrospective review of 40 patients with cancer over a 14-year period that had hemoptysis, the recurrence rate was 20% and cumulative hemoptysis control rate was 0.9 at 1 month and 0.65 at 6 months. 16 This drop-off was acceptable in a patient population with a probability of survival of 0.75 at 1 month and 0.42 at 6 months. Similarly, in a retrospective review of 26 patients with chest malignancy (81% primary lung cancer) and hemoptysis, 12 remote, rebleeding events beyond 6 months were observed in 20% (2/20) compared with an all-cause 6-month mortality rate of 55% (11/20) and an in-hospital mortality rate of 25% (5/20). These results support the durability of TAE for palliation of malignant causes of hemoptysis. In both studies, mortality was high despite embolization, and study design and power were inadequate to determine the effect of embolization on patient survival, but a clear benefit of TAE can be presumed for the indication of massive hemoptysis. Perhaps a clearer measure of the durability of TAE is the hemoptysis-free survival period, which provides patients and referring physicians with realistic expectations during the process of informed consent. Han et al looked specifically at this measure in a retrospective review of 84 patients with hemoptysis caused by primary lung cancer over an 11-year period. 3 Seventy-four patients had NSCLC and 10 had small cell lung cancer. The majority were advanced cases of stage IV lung cancer (53 cases), and a mixture of mild, moderate and massive hemoptysis was included. Like the studies previously mentioned, the recurrence rate during a follow-up period ranging from 3 days to 249 months was 23.8%. The median hemoptysis-free survival period of 61 days was less than the median patient survival period of 99 days in the clinically successful group. Therefore, repeat embolization during the patient''s lifetime should be expected in a minority of patients, and as seen in this study, is typically definitive. Predictive factors of a shorter hemoptysis-free survival period after bland TAE include massive hemoptysis, squamous cell carcinoma, bronchopulmonary shunting 10 and cavitation of lung masses 3 ( Figs. 3 and 5 ). Predictive factors of higher all-cause mortality after embolization include cavitation, 15 intubation status, performance status, advanced stage, hemoglobin at presentation, and the presence of thrombocytopenia. 12 15 In addition to these factors, higher recurrence rates may be associated with missed arterial feeders in cases of multiple feeding vessels 12 and with involvement of the pulmonary arteries, which is uncommon in benign indications for TAE but sporadically described with malignant indications 14 ( Fig. 4 ). Fig. 5. Open in a new tab ( a ) Contrast-enhanced CT shows a large, cavitary squamous cell carcinoma (arrow) occupying the right chest cavity. ( b ) The mass is supplied by an anomalous bronchial artery (arrow) that arises from the aortic arch. ( c ) The bronchial artery was embolized with a combination of tris acryl spheres and coils (arrow). Bland Embolization of Pulmonary Arteries for Refractory Hemoptysis Compared with systemic arterial feeders, pulmonary arteries rarely result in hemoptysis and represent less than 1% of non-neoplastic cases 1 and 6.4% of neoplastic cases in a series of 125 patients with NSCLC. 15 Nevertheless, pulmonary angiography with the intent to embolize may be indicated for patients with recurrent or persistent hemoptysis despite an exhausted search for systemic arterial feeders. Marcelin et al looked specifically at cases of hemoptysis caused by lung malignancy that involved embolization of pulmonary arterial branches after failed TAE. These cases are quite infrequent and required a multicenter, retrospective review of 19 patients with lung malignancy over a 6-year period that had massive or recurrent hemoptysis. Careful planning and verification of pulmonary arterial involvement by CTA prior to embolization is imperative ( Fig. 4 ). In addition, in our experience, cases presenting with possible pulmonary arterial involvement often involve significant persistent bleeding despite previous attempts at TAE and bronchoscopic management. Marcelin et al noted that two patients died during the procedure due to massive hemoptysis and cardiac arrest, and 1 patient was treated with surgery. Primary and assisted technical (and clinical) success rates were 73.7% (14/19) and 84.2% (16/19), respectively. Similar to outcomes for TAE, authors achieved adequate hemostasis during a median follow-up period of 188 days, given the low survival rates of this population (67 and 46% at 1 and 3 months, respectively). In addition to erosion into the pulmonary artery, lung malignancy can result in hemoptysis caused by an associated pulmonary arterial pseudoaneurysm, typically treatable by embolization with coils. 17 TACE for Hemoptysis With an eye toward future applications of transarterial chemoembolization (TACE) for the primary treatment of chest tumors, Seki at al reviewed 10 patients with advanced (stage IIIB/IV) primary lung cancer (squamous cell carcinoma in six, adenocarcinoma in four) who underwent infusion chemotherapy in addition to embolization (chemoembolization) to control tumor-related hemoptysis – mostly moderate. 5 Transarterial infusion chemotherapy via feeding arteries using cisplatin and 5-fluorouracil was repeated every 3–4 weeks for three cycles. HepaSphere (100–150 μm) or gelatin sponge particles were used for embolization just after drug infusion. Results in this small study were quite promising, with a clinical success rate of 100% (resolution in nine, significant decrease in one) and a favorable, median hemoptysis-free survival period of 11.9 months. The potential for TACE as a primary treatment to achieve regression of tumor burden (see below) was evidenced by reduction in size of the target pulmonary lesions in seven patients and resolution of pulmonary atelectasis in three of five patients. TAE for Pre-Operative Embolization Authors have described the use of TAE for preoperative embolization of hypervascular or high-risk chest tumors requiring either open or bronchoscopic resection. Endobronchial tumors, particularly carcinoids, may be highly vascular and result in spontaneously bleeding or bleeding during attempts at endobronchial resection ( Fig. 6 ). Salamonsen et al reviewed 5 patients that underwent TAE to reduce the bleeding risk of endobronchial, laser resection of centrally located carcinoids performed within 48 hours of TAE. 19 Minimal bleeding was noted during the subsequent resection, and the authors suggested a possible role for TAE in these cases to facilitate and improve the safety of endobronchial procedures. Fig. 6. Open in a new tab ( a ) Contrast-enhanced CT shows an endobronchial mass representing a primary bronchogenic carcinoma (arrow) in the left mainstem bronchus with associated atelectasis. The patient presented with massive hemoptysis two weeks after this CT was obtained. Other endobronchial lesions such as carcinoid can have this appearance. ( b ) Selected left bronchial arteriogram shows hypervascularity in the region of the tumor (arrow). ( c ) The bronchial artery was embolized with a combination of tris acryl spheres and coils (arrow). In other case reports, preoperative TAE has been described for resection of a variety of tumors, including a massive, anterior mediastinal thymoma, 20 benign metastasizing leiomyoma of the hilum, 21 bronchial mucoepidermoid, 22 giant fibrous tumor of the pleura, 23 angiosarcoma, 24 and metastasis from endometrial sarcoma. 25 Bland TAE to Achieve Tumor Regression A few case reports demonstrated a possible role in the use of bland TAE to achieve tumor regression and alleviate functional and bulk-related symptoms of benign tumors, 11 26 and a few published series have applied this concept to chest malignancy for the same purpose. 8 TAE has been used to defunctionalize parathyroid adenomas in the aorticopulmonary window, 11 and shrink benign tumors such as Castleman lymphadenopathy, 26 racemose hemangioma, 6 and benign leiomyomas of the hilum. 21 Preliminary CTA, diligent intraprocedural, selective angiography, and intraprocedural cone-beam CT 21 have been applied to ensure that every possible artery is embolized since most benign tumors are supplied by multiple bronchial and nonbronchial feeders. The use of TAE to induce regression of hypervascular chest metastases has been described in larger, retrospective series. Seki et al performed particle embolization of 49 lesions caused by renal cell carcinoma (RCC) (22 pulmonary nodules, 17 mediastinal nodes, and 10 hilar nodes) in 16 patients over 1 to 5 sessions. 8 In these cases, the authors described the use of small particles to achieve a therapeutic, bland embolization. Bronchial arteries were embolized in almost all patients (87.5%) and nonbronchial feeders were included in the majority. According to the Response Evaluation Criteria In Solid Tumors Version 1.1 (RECIST 1.1) criteria, complete response was achieved in 10.2%, 5 partial response in 34.7%, 17 stable disease in 30.6%, 15 and progressive disease in 24.5%. As expected, the greater the enhancement, the greater the response. TPCE to Achieve Tumor Regression Transpulmonary chemoembolization (TPCE) describes injection of chemotherapeutic agents in pulmonary arterial branches supplying unresectable tumor that has been refractory to systemic chemotherapy. The procedure involves balloon occlusion of the feeding segmental branch followed by infusion of chemotherapy agents and then embolization. Vogl et al followed 26 metastatic lesions from various primaries in 23 patients that were treated with TPCE using mitomycin C, iodized oil and microspheres [27]. A mean of 2.8 treatments per patient was performed. As seen with RCC mets treated with bland embolization [8], response correlated with degree of iodized oil enhancement – partial response in 35%, stable disease in 26% and progression in 39%. In a separate, larger study of TPCE for palliation (92 patients) or neoadjuvant treatment before thermal ablation (51 patients) of lung metastases [28], various combinations of chemotherapeutic agents (mitomycin C, gemcitabine, cisplatin and irinotecan) were infused followed by iodized oil and microspheres. A mean of 5.8 treatments per patient was performed. There were no major complications. Response was primarily stabilization of disease – partial response in 12%, stable disease in 66%, and progression in 22%. Mean survival in the palliation and neoadjuvant groups was 19.7 and 30.1 months, respectively, suggesting a greater benefit for the latter indication. TACE and TARE to Achieve Tumor Regression Chen et al applied TACE via the bronchial artery to the definitive treatment of 64 patients with advanced Stage III/IV NSCLC. 27 Thirty-two patients were treated with TACE plus radioactive I-125 seed implantation, and 32 patients were treated with TACE alone, resulting in progression-free survival rates of 12.6 and 8.2 months, local control rates of 90 and 59.3%, and median survival of 644 and 544 days, respectively. These results suggest a benefit to the addition of I-125 seed implantation over TACE alone. In another recent study, Kennoki et al performed TACE of 29 unresectable pulmonary or mediastinal breast cancer metastases in 14 patients over 13 years by injection of a combination of 2 to 4 types of chemotherapy agents such as cisplatin, epirubicin, mitomycin C, and fluorouracil in conjunction with particle embolization. 9 Like the study by Seki et al describing bland embolization of RCC, small particles were used (50–100 μm) to achieve therapeutic embolization in conjunction with the chemotherapy agents, and repeat procedures were performed routinely to achieve a response (mean 5.5 procedures per patient). According to the RECIST 1.1 criteria, complete response was not achieved, partial response was achieved in 28.6% 4 and stable disease in 71.4%. 10 While the study was not comparative and sufficiently powered to determine a survival benefit, the 5-year survival rate was 49% compared with a reported 27% 5-year survival of women with stage IV breast cancer. 28 The authors cited TACE as an option for palliation of chest metastases from breast cancer. Case reports of TACE for other tumor types have been published. Kim et al treated a 46-year-old patient with HCC metastases to the right atrium and lungs with a multidisciplinary regimen of radiotherapy, TACE and sorafenib, which resulted in progressive decrease in tumor burden and finally a complete response as per the modified RECIST criteria. 29 The patient is still alive after 30 months. The reported use of transarterial radioembolization (TARE) for the definitive treatment of chest malignancy is in its infancy, and currently described only in a few case reports. Ricke et al described Y90 TARE of two patients with pulmonary metastases – one from colorectal carcinoma and one from RCC. 30 Y90 resin-microspheres were injected in select regions via the feeding bronchial arteries. According to RECIST 1.1 criteria, both patients showed stable disease or partial remission for the treated lesions compared with progression for the untreated lesions at follow-up durations of less than 12 weeks. Complications Potential complications from TAE applied to chest neoplasms would include previously described complications of TAE for benign indications of the chest such as dysphagia, chest pain, diaphragmatic dysfunction, and neurological, cardiac, or peripheral complications caused by paradoxical embolization of embolic agents through tumor shunting. That said, few complications have been reported, even for larger retrospective series. Since all-cause mortality rates are high and disease processes and treatments are complex for this patient population, the contribution of TAE and related procedures may be difficult to ascertain with the small patient populations reported to date. Intraprocedural death during attempted embolization of uncontrollable, massive hemoptysis has been described. 14 In addition, rare reported complications from TACE of pulmonary neoplasms include rash, chest pain, persistent pleural effusion, and transient chest tightness. 18 Interestingly, no incidents of pulmonary infarction or bronchial necrosis have been described for published cases of TAE, TACE or TARE of chest neoplasms. Summary TAE has evolved from the management of non-neoplastic causes of hemoptysis to a safe and effective treatment for hemoptysis caused by chest tumors. With careful planning using cross-sectional imaging and a thorough angiographic search for feeding vessels, the procedure results in high immediate clinical success and long-term durability that is sufficient for palliation of advanced malignancy. Variations such as TACE and TARE may be feasible options for achieving stabilization or regression of chest malignancies, though published experience with this indication is at its infancy. Early results are promising, and large prospective trials are warranted. References 1. de Gregorio M A, Medrano J, Mainar A, Alfonso E R, Rengel M. [Endovascular treatment of massive hemoptysis by bronchial artery embolization: short-term and long-term follow-up over a 15-year period] Arch Bronconeumol. 2006;42(02):49–56. doi: 10.1016/s1579-2129(06)60117-1. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Jesudasan C D, Nair S, Thangakunam B, Keshava S N, Moses V. Near-fatal hemorrhage after bronchoscopic resection of a carcinoid tumor: successful management by bronchial artery embolization. J Bronchology Interv Pulmonol. 2010;17(02):152–154. doi: 10.1097/LBR.0b013e3181da2faf. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Han K, Yoon K W, Kim J H, Kim G M. Bronchial artery embolization for hemoptysis in primary lung cancer: a retrospective review of 84 patients. J Vasc Interv Radiol. 2019;30(03):428–434. doi: 10.1016/j.jvir.2018.08.022. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Fujita T, Tanabe M, Moritani K, Matsunaga N, Matsumoto T. Immediate and late outcomes of bronchial and systemic artery embolization for palliative treatment of patients with nonsmall-cell lung cancer having hemoptysis. Am J Hosp Palliat Care. 2014;31(06):602–607. doi: 10.1177/1049909113499442. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Seki A, Shimono C. Transarterial chemoembolization for management of hemoptysis: initial experience in advanced primary lung cancer patients. Jpn J Radiol. 2017;35(09):495–504. doi: 10.1007/s11604-017-0659-2. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Fujimoto K, Kasai H, Sugiura T, Tanabe N, Tatsumi K. Primary racemose haemangioma of the right bronchial artery with thrombus and slow meandering blood flow. Thorax. 2017;72(10):962–963. doi: 10.1136/thoraxjnl-2016-209668. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Kucukay F, Melih Topcuoglu O, Alpar A, Murat Altay C et al. Bronchial artery embolization with large sized (700–900 μm) tris-acryl microspheres (Embosphere) for massive hemoptysis: long-term results. Cardiovasc Intervent Radiol. 2018;41:225–230. doi: 10.1007/s00270-017-1818-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Seki A, Hori S, Sueyoshi S et al. Transcatheter arterial embolization with spherical embolic agent for pulmonary metastases from renal cell carcinoma. Cardiovasc Intervent Radiol. 2013;36(06):1527–1535. doi: 10.1007/s00270-013-0576-4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Kennoki N, Hori S, Yuki T, Hori A. Transcatheter arterial chemoembolization with spherical embolic agent in patients with pulmonary or mediastinal metastases from breast cancer. J Vasc Interv Radiol. 2017;28(10):1386–1394. doi: 10.1016/j.jvir.2017.06.003. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Chen J, Chen L A, Liang Z X et al. Immediate and long-term results of bronchial artery embolization for hemoptysis due to benign versus malignant pulmonary diseases. Am J Med Sci. 2014;348(03):204–209. doi: 10.1097/MAJ.0000000000000226. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Ali M, Kumpe D A. Embolization of bronchial artery-supplied ectopic parathyroid adenomas located in the aortopulmonary window. J Vasc Interv Radiol. 2014;25(01):138–143. doi: 10.1016/j.jvir.2013.10.008. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Mehta A S, Ahmed O, Jilani D et al. Bronchial artery embolization for malignant hemoptysis: a single institutional experience. J Thorac Dis. 2015;7(08):1406–1413. doi: 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2015.07.39. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Kato A, Kudo S, Matsumoto K et al. Bronchial artery embolization for hemoptysis due to benign diseases: immediate and long-term results. Cardiovasc Intervent Radiol. 2000;23(05):351–357. doi: 10.1007/s002700010062. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Marcelin C, Soussan J, Desmots F et al. Outcomes of pulmonary artery embolization and stent graft placement for the treatment of hemoptysis caused by lung tumors. J Vasc Interv Radiol. 2018;29(07):975–980. doi: 10.1016/j.jvir.2018.01.773. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Razazi K, Parrot A, Khalil A et al. Sev